dule. Plast Reconstr Surg. 2009;124(3):952–954. doi: 10.1097/PRS.0b013e3181b17bf5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Contag SP, Klein AM, Blount AC. Johns MM 3rd.Validation of a laryngeal dissection module for phonomicrosurgical training. Laryngoscope. 2009;119(1):211–215. doi: 10.1002/lary.20018. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Bridges M, Diamond DL. The financial impact of teaching surgical residents in the operating room. Am J Surg. 1999;177:28–32. doi: 10.1016/s0002-9610(98)00289-x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Glaser A, Hall C, Uribe J, et al. The Effects of Previously Acquired Skills on Sinus Surgery Simulator Performance. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2005:525–530. doi: 10.1016/j.otohns.2005.06.022. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Tsai CL, Heinrichs WL. Acquisition of eye-hand coordination skills for videoendoscopic surgery. J Am Assoc Gynecol Laparosc. 1994;1(4,Part 2):S37. doi: 10.1016/s1074-3804(05)80989-2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',26,'2025-10-14 12:59:36','2025-10-14 12:59:36');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2994,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10900716','Application of the combination of CBL teaching method and SEGUE framework to improve the doctor-patient communication skills of resident physicians in otolaryngology department.','Zeng N, Lu H, Li S, Yang Q, Liu F, Pan H, Yan S.','2024','popularization embodies harmonious constructivism huazhong nanshan makoul torhinolaryngology lingping jargon overemphasis overlooking undervalued impatience impatience otoscopes mastered mastered untrained convincible shuo qiong hongguang b2022097 huazhong makoul s0738 suchman makoul neth s0738 gorawara betancourt grantcharov reznick','Abstract Background To explore the feasibility and effectiveness of applying CBL teaching method and SEGUE Framework in the doctor-patient communication skills of resident physicians in the department of otolaryngology. Methods This is an observational study to compare the score changes in doctor-patient communication skills of 120 resident physicians, before and after using CBL combined SEGUE Framework teaching method. The effects of gender, age, grade, educational background and marital status on SEGUE score were analyzed. Results Through the combined application of CBL teaching method and SEGUE Framework, the SEGUE score of 120 resident physicians was significantly improved. There was no significant difference in SEGUE score among different sex and marital status of resident physicians. SEGUE score is positively correlated with age; Different grades and educational backgrounds have significant effects on SEGUE score. Conclusion The combination of CBL teaching method and SEGUE Framework is feasible and effective in the education program of doctor-patient communication skills for resident physicians in the department of otolaryngology, and worthy of popularization and application in other medical specialties. Keywords: CBL teaching method, SEGUE framework, Department of otolaryngology, Standardized resident training, Doctor-patient communication Background Doctor-patient communication, in form, refers to the bidirectional exchange of information between healthcare professionals and patients and/or their family members during the diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up processes. In essence, it embodies the emotional communication and establishment of trust between the doctor and the patient [ 1 ]. In the current biopsychosocial model of medicine, proficient communication skills are essential for building harmonious doctor-patient relationships and addressing clinical issues. The lack of communication between doctors and patients can lead to patient doubts regarding the doctor’s explanation of the condition and treatment plan, as well as increase the difficulty of diagnosis and treatment for doctors. Among the defects in the medical education of our country, one particular issue is that the education on ethics and humanities for medical students is lacking, which would hinder their effective communication with patients and also affect their professional skills and overall qualities. Otorhinolaryngology is one important medical discipline, but its complexity, diversity, specificity, and limitations in teaching methods and resources, bring several challenges to medical education. To optimize the training quality for students in this discipline, Case-Based Learning (CBL) and SEGUE Framework, two newly emerging tools for medical education, might hold great promise [ 2 – 3 ]. CBL is a teaching approach based on case studies that requires students to independently solve problems and make decisions [ 2 ]. In the context of medical education, it plays a crucial role in developing students’ clinical reasoning, problem-solving skills, and the ability to apply theoretical knowledge to real-world medical scenarios. CBL’s theoretical basis in constructivism, problem-based learning, and experiential learning aligns with the core principles of medical education. It offers a pedagogical approach that prepares future healthcare professionals to think critically, solve problems effectively, and provide patient-centered care. While, the SEGUE Framework is an assessment tool to evaluate communication skills, providing a systematic approach to guide physicians in effective doctor-patient communication [ 3 ]. As such, the SEGUE Framework is another valuable tool in medical education, helping to foster the development of well-rounded, competent, and compassionate physicians. Considering the unique characteristic of otorhinolaryngology and the poor current state of medical education in this discipline, it is of great importance to integrate these teaching methods and assess its efficacy. In this study, we aim to explore the effectiveness of integrating CBL with the SEGUE Framework in improving the communication skills of resident physicians in otorhinolaryngology training programs (for short “residency training”). Method Study participants We conducted an observational study involving a statistical population currently undergoing normative residency training and selected our sample using a systematic random sampling method. A total of 120 physicians undergoing otorhinolaryngology residency training at 9 training centers in Shenzhen City from January 2022 to June 2022 were selected as the study participants. The inclusion criteria were as follows: ① Second and third-year otorhinolaryngology residents in training; ② First-year residents who had completed at least one month of rotation in the otorhinolaryngology department at their respective training centers. Exclusion criteria included residents who withdrew from the training program for personal reasons. This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of Huazhong University of Science and Technology Union Shenzhen Hospital (Nanshan Hospital). All participating residents who completed the questionnaire were informed and gave their consent for their involvement in the study. Research tools SEGUE Communication Skills Assessment Tool: The SEGUE Framework is a model designed to guide medical students in effective communication with patients. It was developed by Professor Makoul from Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine in the United States [ 4 ]. In 2006, it was introduced and adapted into Chinese by China Medical University [ 5 ]. Research has shown that SEGUE can effectively measure and evaluate the communication skills of Chinese medical students, demonstrating good reliability and validity. SEGUE is an acronym derived from the first letters of several English words, representing the following components: S (Set the Stage): Creating a comfortable environment and establishing trust with patients, making them feel respected and cared for; E (Elicit Information): Guiding patients to actively share their medical history, symptoms, including the duration of illness, location of pain, and nature of symptoms, among others; G (Give Information): Presenting diagnosis results, disease characteristics, treatment plans, and other relevant information to patients in easily understandable language; U (Understand the Patient’s Perspective): Understanding the patient’s perspective on the disease, their attitude towards the treatment plan, and their expectations for treatment outcomes, in order to better serve the patient; E (End the Encounter): Summarizing the discussion, confirming the next steps and action plans with the patient, and ensuring that the patient feels that their concerns have been addressed and attended to. The SEGUE Framework is a structured and systematic communication guide model that helps healthcare professionals communicate effectively with patients and achieve better clinical outcomes. It has been widely used in medical education and clinical practice. Moreover, SEGUE is also a very good assessing tool to evaluate the communication performance [ 4 ]. Research methods This study commenced in January 2022. Prior to the start of the study, the 120 participating residents received varying levels of theoretical knowledge and practical training in otorhinolaryngology, including departmental education, teaching rounds, and simulated scenarios for medical communication. SEGUE is not only a teaching tool but also an assessing method to evaluate the communication skills. Therefore the SEGUE questionnaire was utilized here to collect data from participants. In January 2022, the first around of survey was conducted using the SEGUE questionnaire. Subsequently, over a six-month period, an expert group composed of teaching and management faculty in otorhinolaryngology from the nine training centers selected cases for CBL based on the requirements outlined in the “Standardized Training for Resident Physicians (2021 Edition) - Otorhinolaryngology Training Guidelines.” These cases were combined with the SEGUE framework and included specific medical communication scenarios for training purposes. In June 2022, the SEGUE questionnaire survey was conducted again. The data from two rounds of surveys based on SEGUE questionnaires were further extracted, summarized and analyzed for comparison. Statistical analysis Data analysis was performed using SPSS 22.0 statistical software. After checking the normality of the metric data, descriptive statistics were presented as ‾X ± s , and paired t-tests or analysis of variance (ANOVA) were employed to determine the statistical difference between groups. Pearson correlation analysis was used for assessing correlations. A two-tailed test was conducted, and a P- value of < 0.05 was considered statistically significant for detecting differences. Results Through the combination of CBL teaching method and SEGUE framework, the SEGUE scores of 120 residents were significantly improved. The specific content of the score table is analyzed as follows. General information and comparison of SEGUE scores among resident physicians A total of 120 questionnaires were distributed in this study, and all 120 questionnaires were returned, resulting in a response rate of 100%. Overall, 68 males and 52 females were involved, and their age ranged from 23 to 36 years, with an average of 26.13 (± 2.09) years. There were 42 first-year residents, 50 s-year residents, and 28 third-year residents. In terms of education background, 15 residents had a bachelor’s degree, 90 had a master’s degree, and 15 had a doctoral degree. Among the participants, 108 were unmarried and 12 were married. The average SEGUE score prior to training was 77.38 (± 3.78), while the average score improved to 85.91 (± 2.82) after six months of training. A statistically significant difference was observed ( P < 0.05). Comparison of SEGUE scores among resident physicians of different genders The comparison of SEGUE scores among resident physicians of different genders showed no statistically significant difference ( P < 0.05). Please refer to Table 1 . Table 1. Comparison of SEGUE scores of resident physicians across genders, training years, educational background and marital status Group Index Number Average score before training Average score after training P value Gender Male 68 77.28 86.00 0.750 Female 52 77.50 85.79 Training Year First Year 42 75.48 84.98 0.000 Second Year 50 77.66 85.98 Third Year 18 79.71 87.18 Education background Undergraduate 15 77.20 85.67 0.000 Master 90 76.77 85.44 Doctor 15 81.20 88.93 Marital status Unmarried 108 77.16 85.68 0.053 Married 12 79.33 88.00 Open in a new tab Comparison of SEGUE scores of resident physicians across different ages Pearson correlation analysis revealed a positive correlation between SEGUE scores of resident physicians and their age. The correlation coefficients (r) for the two assessments were 0.338 and 0.333, respectively ( P < 0.05). Please refer to Table 2 . Table 2. Comparison of correlation between SEGUE scores and age among resident physicians of different ages Group Pearson Correlation P value Before training 0.338** 0.000 After training 0.333** 0.000 Open in a new tab Comparison of SEGUE scores of resident physicians among different training years Levene’s test revealed unequal variances in SEGUE scores among resident physicians of different training years. The results showed significant differences in SEGUE scores among different training years during the first assessment ( P < 0.05). After the combined training using CBL methodology and SEGUE Framework, the overall SEGUE scores of the 120 resident physicians significantly improved ( P < 0.05). However, there were no significant differences in scores between the second and third-year trainees. Please refer to Table 1 . Comparison of SEGUE scores among resident physicians with different educational backgrounds Levene’s test revealed heterogeneity of variance in SEGUE scores among resident physicians with different educational backgrounds. One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) test was used for analyzing the data, and the results showed that after the combined application of CBL teaching method and SEGUE Framework, the SEGUE scores of resident physicians with different educational backgrounds significantly improved ( P < 0.05). However, there was no significant difference in SEGUE scores between master’s degree and undergraduate resident physicians in the two assessments. Please refer to Table 1 . Comparison of SEGUE scores among resident physicians with different marital status The comparison of SEGUE scores among resident physicians with different marital statuses before and after the training showed a significant statistical difference ( P < 0.05). However, when comparing the scores for each assessment, there was no significant statistical difference observed among resident physicians with different marital statuses. This indicates that marital status does not have a significant statistical impact on SEGUE scores. Please refer to Table 1 . Discussion Current status of doctor-patient communication skills among torhinolaryngology resident physicians The results of this assessment revealed that the average SEGUE score before training was (77.38 ± 3.78) points, while the score after six months of training was (85.91 ± 2.82) points. The difference between the two scores was statistically significant ( ρ < 0.05), indicating a significant improvement in scores after the training. Comparing these results with the data on doctor-patient communication skills among resident physicians by Zhu Lingping, Zhu Dan, and others [ 6 – 7 ], it is evident that the otorhinolaryngology resident physicians in the training bases need further enhancement of their doctor-patient communication skills in future training. Otorhinolaryngology is an independent secondary discipline in clinical medicine. It is highly specialized and involves a wide range of complex course content, encompassing multiple interdisciplinary areas. However, it receives relatively few hours of instruction during undergraduate medical education, and the practical training is not extensive. This lack of in-depth understanding and memorization difficulties pose significant challenges in teaching [ 8 ]. Therefore, during the residency training stage, clinical education in otorhinolaryngology faces enormous pressure and emphasizes practical skills and clinical reasoning, ultimately leading to more effective doctor-patient communication.Based on extensive clinical observations, otorhinolaryngology resident physicians often face difficulties in effectively communicating with patients, which can be summarized as follows: ① Frequent use of medical terminology: When communicating with patients, otorhinolaryngology resident physicians may use excessive medical jargon, making it difficult for patients to understand and accept the information [ 9 ]. ② Overemphasis on medical condition, neglecting emotional needs of patients: Resident physicians may focus excessively on the medical condition and treatment, overlooking the emotional needs of patients, which can make them feel undervalued. ③ Lack of communication skills: Resident physicians may lack essential communication skills such as active listening, effective expression, appropriate questioning, and providing feedback. This can result in poor communication outcomes and an inability to establish a good doctor-patient relationship [ 10 ]. ④ Impatience with patients: Otorhinolaryngology resident physicians may demonstrate impatience with patients who have complex medical conditions, which hinders their ability to effectively explain the diagnosis and treatment, leading to a loss of patient confidence [ 11 ]. ⑤ Lack of cultural sensitivity: When communicating with patients, it is important to consider their cultural background and values. Without cultural sensitivity, misunderstandings or negative reactions may occur [ 12 ]. ⑥ Insufficient skill development: Resident physicians need to master various technical skills in clinical practice, such as using laryngoscopes, nasal endoscopes, and otoscopes. Without effective skill training, it is challenging to improve medical competence and service quality [ 13 ]. After six months of training using a combination of CBL, the SEGUE framework, and incorporating special doctor-patient communication scenarios, there was a significant improvement in the SEGUE scores of the 120 resident physicians. Based on the analysis and summary, during the clinical teaching process, the instructors provided resident physicians with typical cases for discussion and analysis. By studying these cases, resident physicians gained an understanding of how to effectively communicate with patients using the SEGUE framework and acquired specific skills and techniques.The second round of SEGUE scores also demonstrated the significant effectiveness of the combined application of CBL and the SEGUE framework. Through case-based learning and discussions, resident physicians not only mastered basic medical knowledge and skills but also learned how to effectively communicate with patients. They learned how to listen to patients’ needs and concerns, ask clear questions, provide concise explanations, and reach consensus with patients.By combining CBL and the SEGUE framework, resident physicians can continuously improve their medical knowledge and doctor-patient communication skills through practical experiences. They can gain in-depth understanding of various diseases and clinical situations, enhance their clinical reasoning abilities, and improve their problem-solving skills. Simultaneously, resident physicians can communicate more effectively with patients, establish a good doctor-patient relationship, and improve patient treatment outcomes and satisfaction. Factors influencing the doctor-patient communication skills of resident physicians In this study, different analyses were conducted based on gender, age, grade, educational background, and marital status. The results indicate that there were no significant differences in SEGUE scores among resident physicians of different genders and marital statuses. SEGUE scores were positively correlated with age. Different grades and educational backgrounds had a significant impact on SEGUE scores.In the initial evaluation, SEGUE scores increased with higher grades, and the second evaluation also showed that second- and third-year residents had significantly higher scores than first-year residents. In both evaluations, doctoral students had significantly higher SEGUE scores than master’s students and undergraduate students, while there was no significant difference in SEGUE scores between master’s students and undergraduate students.The analysis suggests that master’s students and undergraduate students received similar clinical practice guidance and theoretical training in otolaryngology before entering the specialized base. They were of similar ages during their residency training. As resident physicians accumulated clinical practice experience and training time, their doctor-patient communication skills gradually improved. Based on a certain level of clinical practice, resident physicians gradually mastered the process and content of doctor-patient communication, that is, knowing what to say and how to say it. This enables them to gradually convey information to patients that they may not be aware of, thereby increasing mutual trust and cooperation between doctors and patients [ 14 ]. Reflection on the evaluation process of doctor-patient communication skills in resident physicians Doctor-patient communication skills consist of basic language abilities as well as comprehensive communication skills, linguistic adaptability, and humanistic care abilities with the characteristics of the medical profession [ 15 ]. In modern society, doctors and patients are burdened with more complex factors such as economics, politics, culture, environment, and psychology. Therefore, medical practice requires a continuous strengthening of the thinking and behavior of doctor-patient communication [ 16 ]. In the new environment, healthcare professionals need the active participation of patients in both subjective and objective aspects of the medical process. Inquiry preparation, information gathering, and information provision all rely on the active cooperation of patients and their families. Regarding the current status of doctor-patient communication in otolaryngology resident physicians, in order to improve the learning outcomes and clinical practice abilities of resident physicians and facilitate effective communication, we can establish a teaching model centered around resident physicians. Resident physicians can participate in case analysis, diagnosis, treatment, and other processes, allowing them to solve practical problems through their own thinking and exploration. This approach not only enables resident physicians to acquire basic medical knowledge and skills but also helps them understand how to effectively communicate with patients. They learn how to listen to the needs and concerns of patients, how to ask precise questions, how to provide clear explanations, and how to reach consensus with patients. One limitation of this study is the lack of a control group, who did not receive the combined-method training. The biggest difficulty preventing us to do so was that multiple centers were involved in our study. It was not plausible to take participants from some institutes as a control while the others as the treatment group as it would also create bias. On the other hand, it was unreasonable to divide participants from one single institute into different groups (the trained and untrained) since we were concerned that they might affect each other. However, to perform a more convincible study, we can design an experiment in the future with a larger sample size so that the participants can randomly divided into two groups in each clinical center, with the treatment group receiving the CBL-SEGUE Framework teaching method while the control receiving the traditional teaching method. But the interaction between them should be prohibited. Conclusion Clinical teaching for otolaryngology resident physicians faces numerous challenges, requiring collaborative efforts from medical schools and teachers to explore better teaching methods and strategies. Combining the CBL approach with the SEGUE Framework proves to be a highly effective method for teaching and communication, enabling resident physicians to enhance their medical knowledge and improve their doctor-patient communication skills in the field of otolaryngology. Furthermore, it also enhances their self-learning abilities and self-management skills. Therefore, this approach is worth promoting and applying in other medical specialties. Acknowledgements Thank every author for his work. Author contributions Nan Zeng, Hui Lu and Fei Liu wrote the main manuscript text, Shuo Li, Qiong Yang and Hongguang Pan were responsible for the training of all participants in the paper, and Shang Yan was responsible for reviewing the manuscript. All authors reviewed the manuscript. Funding Supported by Guangdong High-level Hospital Construction Fund and Medical Scientific Research Foundation of Guangdong Province of China(B2022097). Data availability The datasets used and analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate The study complied with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki on Biomedical Research Involving Human Subjects and was approved by the Ethics Review Board of Huazhong University of Science and Technology Union Shenzhen Hospital (protocol number 202204002). Written informed consent was obtained from all subjects. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Liu B, Teng Z. Analysis of the Concept of Doctor-Patient communication. Chin J Med Ethics. 2010;23(06):83–5. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Zhao W, He L, Deng W, et al. The effectiveness of the combined problem-based learning (PBL) and case-based learning (CBL) teaching method in the clinical practical teaching of thyroid disease. BMC Med Educ. 2020;20(1):381. doi: 10.1186/s12909-020-02306-y. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Che N, Zhang J, Huang X, et al. Application of SEGUE Scale-based doctor-patient communication skills training in interns and regular trainees. Volume 312. Chinese Higher Medical Education; 2022. pp. 77–9. 12. 4. Makoul G. The SEGUE Framework for teaching and assessing communication skills. Patient Educ Couns. 2001;45(1):23–34. doi: 10.1016/S0738-3991(01)00136-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Li JA. Study on the Evaluation of Medical Students’ Doctor-Patient Communication Skills Using the SEGUE Scale. [Doctoral dissertation]. China Medical University.2008. 6. Zhu L, Li Y, Chen H, et al. Exploration of the combination of CBL Teaching Method and SEGUE Scale to improve the Doctor-Patient Communication Skills of Pediatric Interns. Chin J Continuing Med Educ. 2021;13(28):56–60. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Zhu D, Liu D, Feng X, et al. Analysis of the Assessment of Doctor-Patient Communication Skills in Resident Physicians using the SEGUE Scale. Chin J Clin Physicians (Electronic Edition) 2016;10(23):3678–82. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Wang Q, Liu Y, Wu K, et al. The role of anatomy in the introductory teaching of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck surgery for Physicians. Chin Gen Pract. 2019;17(05):844–7. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Suchman AL, Roter D, Green M, et al. Physician satisfaction with primary care office visits. Collaborative Study Group of the American Academy on Physician and Patient. Med Care. 1993;31(12):1083–92. doi: 10.1097/00005650-199312000-00002. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Makoul G, Schofield T. Communication teaching and assessment in medical education: an international consensus statement. Neth Inst Prim Health Care Patient Educ Couns. 1999;37(2):191–5. doi: 10.1016/S0738-3991(99)00023-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Levinson W, Gorawara-Bhat R, Lamb J. A study of patient clues and physician responses in primary care and surgical settings. JAMA 2000 Aug 23–30;284(8):1021–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 12. Betancourt JR, Green AR, Carrillo JE, et al. Defining cultural competence: a practical framework for addressing racial/ethnic disparities in health and health care. Public Health Rep. 2003 Jul-Aug;118(4):293–302. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 13. Grantcharov TP, Reznick RK. Teaching procedural skills. BMJ. 2008;336(7653):1129–31. doi: 10.1136/bmj.39517.686956.47. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Guo F, Wang Y. Application of CBL combined with scenario Simulation Micro-lectures based on SPOC in Doctor-Patient Communication Teaching for Resident Physicians. China Med Educ Technol 2020,34(01):36–40. 15. Lin A, Liu H. Analysis of G-factor and S-factor in doctor-patient communication skills. Med Philos (A) 2015;36(06):52–4. [ Google Scholar ] 16. Li Z, Leng M. Review of Research on Doctor-Patient Relationship. Chin Hosp Manage. 2009;29(03):40–3. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement The datasets used and analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',78,'2025-10-14 12:59:46','2025-10-14 12:59:46');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2995,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12332797','Artificial intelligence in pediatric otolaryngology: A state-of-the-art review of opportunities and pitfalls.','Navarathna N, Kanhere A, Gomez C, Isaiah A.','2025','hierarchically excelling compressing hyperparameter refines tradeoff penalizes penalizing partitioned shapley shap approximating shap hovers otoscopic textures rnns rnns sundgaard wideband tympanometry binol tympanometry otoscopic cephalometry biosignals oxinet oxinet perceptron crowson nasopharyngoscopy 90s crowson otoscopes otoscope otoscope jongbloed tonsillectomies untapped elude audiometric conventions convincingly generalizing underrepresent dialect accents tones perturbing precedence fortify r01hl167012 amal isaiah roepman dietmann virta kengara tiedje xinn lecun nature14539 crowson ranisau eskander babier kahmke tcy goshtasbi 93t947gh sarrami gatidis varniab chaudhari daldrup e2431076 varoquaux colliot colliot nbk597473 warfield bharatha tempany cmc kaus haker jolesz kikinis s1076 schober schwarte charilaou battat v28 guestrin sigkdd knowl neurips paper_files hash 8a20a8621978632d76c43dfd28b67767 blomgren pitkaranta sunwoo bioeng switz bioengineering10111337 crowson hartnick sundgaard zafer fusing biocybern bbe neunet kovacevic condessa shope haralam campese cavdar hoberman sundgaard laugesen harte kamide wideband tympanometry jbhi binol moberly niazi mkk essig elmaraghy teknos taj schaal gurcan tympanometry eardrum spie cephalometry diagnostics11081386 piotto gambadauro rocchi lelii madini cerrato chironi belhaj patria switz children10060955 garde hoppenbrouwer dehkordi rollinson wensley ansermino hypopnea modularized s336344 behar multilayer perceptron ymeth crowson gipson kadosh hartnick grealish keamy kinane hartnick e13851 jsr xiu tuohuti hypopnea s492146 rajesh asaad saadoun jeevakala sreelakshmi rangasami sivaprakasam s11548 crowson hartnick diercks fracchia setlur e2020034546 rawal jehp jehp_402_23 e2344 e2351 otoscope ohn surapaneni marston vecchiotti rangarajan valdez frosolini caragli genovese filippis marioni s24227126 jongbloed s0022215124001154 rothka hearn meci labarge walen slonimsky mcginn moise centomo bozzo orishchak alnoury moise centomo bozzo orishchak alnoury children10101634 szeliski mcmahan ramage hampson arcas pmlr v54 mcmahan17a bozoki recognit patcog 06683v6 lipkova dfk gigapixel landsman fasano mccarron schoffstall witkin cpp0000484 pronk merkus goverts anema e0208797 rameau eufos affil frosolini caragli genovese filippis marioni s24227126','Abstract Background: Artificial Intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) have transformative potential in enhancing diagnostics, treatment planning, and patient management. However, their application in pediatric otolaryngology remains limited as the unique physiological and developmental characteristics of children require tailored AI applications, highlighting a gap in knowledge. Purpose: To provide a narrative review of current literature on the application of AI in pediatric otolaryngology, highlighting knowledge gaps, associated challenges and future directions. Results: ML models have demonstrated efficacy in diagnosing conditions such as otitis media, adenoid hypertrophy, and pediatric obstructive sleep apnea through deep learning-based image analysis and predictive modeling. AI systems also show potential in surgical settings such as landmark identification during otologic surgery and prediction of middle ear effusion during tympanostomy tube placement. Telemedicine solutions and large language models have shown potential to improve accessibility to care and patient education. The principal challenges include flawed generalization of adult training data and the relative lack of pediatric data. Conclusions: AI holds significant promise in pediatric otolaryngology. However, its widespread clinical integration requires addressing algorithmic bias, enhancing model interpretability, and ensuring robust validation across diverse pediatric populations. Future research should prioritize federated learning, developmental trajectory modeling, and psychosocial integration to create patient-centered solutions. Keywords: Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Deep Learning, Pediatric Otolaryngology 1. Introduction Artificial intelligence (AI) broadly refers to algorithms capable of performing typical human tasks such as decision-making, pattern recognition, and problem-solving. Machine learning (ML), a subset of AI, enables computers to learn from data and improve their performance over time without explicit programming ( Fig. 1 ). Deep learning (DL), a subset of ML, utilizes artificial neural networks inspired by the human brain. These networks consist of multiple layers of interconnected nodes that process data hierarchically, allowing them to detect complex patterns within large datasets [ 1 , 2 ]. DL has been transformative in medical applications, excelling in tasks such as medical image analysis, natural language processing, and predictive analytics [ 3 ]. Clinical machine learning frameworks systematically process multiple data modalities through a series of analytical stages to extract relevant features from the data to predict health outcomes ( Fig. 2 ). Figure 1. Hierarchical overview of major machine learning paradigms. Open in a new tab Supervised Learning operates on fully labeled data, typically for classification (predicting discrete labels) or regression (predicting continuous values). Semi‐Supervised learning incorporates a small portion of labeled data alongside unlabeled data - often with pseudo‐labeling - to improve model training. Unsupervised Learning relies on unlabeled data to uncover patterns, exemplified by clustering (grouping similar points) and dimensionality reduction (compressing features while retaining core information). Finally, Reinforcement Learning optimizes an agent’s actions in an environment through reward signals to guide sequential decision-making. Figure 2. Workflow for developing predictive health models from diverse data sources. Open in a new tab Genomic, clinical note, imaging, and physiological datasets are first curated and integrated into a Clinical Database. Relevant features are then extracted for input to the Model Training process. Next, Model Optimization (e.g., hyperparameter tuning, validation) refines the model’s performance. Finally, the trained model delivers Predicted Health Outcomes, assisting in clinical decision‐making and patient care. Created in https://BioRender.com . Over the past decade, pediatric otolaryngology has experienced a shift toward data-informed decision-making. Modern healthcare environments generate extensive clinical data, ranging from digital otoscopy images and polysomnography results to electronic health records. This vast output offers an opportunity to discover patterns not readily discernible through conventional approaches such as statistical learning. In pediatric otolaryngology, conditions such as otitis media and obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) present significant unresolved heterogeneity. Therefore, ML holds significant promise in supporting precision medicine. Despite the growing number of AI-based studies in in general otolaryngology, pediatric applications remain relatively scarce. Much of the existing literature is based on adult patient populations, such as diagnostic algorithms for head and neck cancers [ 4 ] or predictive models for postoperative complications [ 5 ], which do not translate directly to children. The inherent physiological and developmental differences in children require adaptation of existing AI approaches and, in many cases, the creation of entirely new ML methods [ 6 ]. Furthermore, limited pediatric datasets and ethical concerns regarding the collection and use of children’s medical data underscore the critical need for structured, integrative reviews of AI applications. In response to these knowledge gaps, this review aims to evaluate key AI and ML contributions to pediatric otolaryngology, identifying areas where these algorithms have influenced clinical workflows and highlighting persistent limitations. We emphasize the technical underpinnings of ML solutions and the real-world demonstrations of AI in children, addressing concerns such as interpretability of complex models and data quality. We also seek to illuminate future opportunities for translational research, ensuring that emerging computational methods align with ethical standards. Ultimately, this review endeavors to offer both a critical synthesis of contemporary findings and a forward-looking perspective on the next wave of AI-driven innovations. 2. Model Evaluation and Outcome Measures Evaluating the performance of AI systems in pediatric otolaryngology requires rigorous assessment frameworks appropriate for clinical context and implementation. A summary of key metrics used to assess these models is outlined in Table 1 . Classification tasks, such as presence or absence of pathology, involve categorical outcomes [ 7 ]. A confusion matrix is a tabular visualization that summarizes the performance of a classification model by showing the counts of true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives. True positives and true negatives, more commonly known as sensitivity and specificity, measure the AI system’s ability to correctly identify patients with the pathology from those without. In addition to the metrics derived from the confusion matrix, balanced accuracy is a metric used to provide a more representative measure of performance because standard accuracy can be misleading when disease conditions have low prevalence. Lastly, the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve evaluates classification performance across various threshold settings and offers an overview of the tradeoff between sensitivity and specificity. Higher values indicate better discrimination. Table 1. Summary of AI performance metrics. This table outlines key evaluation metrics used to assess AI models in pediatric otolaryngology, including classification, segmentation, and regression tasks. Classification metrics, such as sensitivity, specificity, and AUC-ROC, evaluate the model’s ability to distinguish between disease and non-disease states. The Dice score quantifies segmentation accuracy for medical imaging applications. Regression metrics, including MAE, RMSE, and R 2 , all measure the accuracy of AI models predicting continuous variables. Evaluation Type Metric Significance Classification Sensitivity (True Positive Rate) Ability to correctly identify patients with the pathology. Specificity (True Negative Rate) Ability to correctly identify patients without the pathology. Positive Predictive Value (PPV) Probability that a positive test truly indicates disease. Negative Predictive Value (NPV) Probability that a negative test truly rules out disease. Balanced Accuracy Adjusted measure of accuracy for imbalanced datasets. Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC) Evaluates model performance across different thresholds; higher values indicate better discrimination ability. Segmentation DICE Coefficient Measures overlap between predicted and actual segmented regions in medical images. Prediction Mean Absolute Error (MAE) Measures the average magnitude of prediction errors. Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) Similar to MAE but penalizes larger errors more heavily. R 2 (Coefficient of Determination) Indicates how well predictions explain variance in actual values. Correlation Coefficients (Pearson, Spearman) Measures strength of association between predicted and actual values. Open in a new tab While not commonly used for traditional classification tasks, the Dice score is a metric essential for clinical image segmentation tasks. Dice score is a similarity coefficient that assesses the overlap between predicted regions and ground truth regions, ranging from 0 (no overlap) to 1 (perfect overlap) [ 8 ]. For models predicting continuous variables, different metrics apply for measures of predictive accuracy. The mean absolute error and the root mean squared error quantify the average magnitude of prediction errors with the latter penalizing performance by the magnitude of errors. R 2 values quantify AI model’s predictions fit the actual values, with values ranging from 0 to 1, where 1 indicates that the model perfectly explains all the variability in the outcome data. Correlation metrics such as Pearson’s or Spearman’s coefficients [ 9 ] measure the strength of association between predicted and actual values. Despite advances in AI-driven diagnostics, several challenges remain. Overfitting, where models perform well on training data but fail to generalize to new patients can be challenging where large, diverse datasets are unavailable [ 10 ]. Two techniques are implemented to detect and mitigate overfitting: cross-validation, where existing data is partitioned into subsets and evaluated on how it performs on unseen data, and external validation, which evaluates performance using new data that was not used during the model’s development. An additional challenge is that many high-performing DL models operate as “black boxes,” where the decision-making process remains opaque. The inability to determine which features most strongly influence model predictions limits clinical interpretability and hinders clinicians’ trust in incorporating AI recommendations into their decision-making. To address these issues, model interpretability techniques such as Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) [ 11 ] and SHapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) [ 12 ] can be employed. LIME provides instance-based explanations by approximating a model’s behavior using simpler surrogate models, while SHAP quantifies the contribution of individual features to a model’s predictions. 3. Clinical Applications 3.1. Diagnostic Support Systems AI can enhance diagnostic accuracy in conditions such as otitis media, adenoid hypertrophy, and OSA. Several DL-based approaches, specifically computer vision methods, have demonstrated superior performance in identifying and classifying these conditions. For otitis media, traditional diagnostic accuracy hovers around 80% even among experienced physicians [ 13 ]. Differentiating acute otitis media (AOM) from otitis media with effusion (OME) is particularly challenging [ 14 ]. This complexity presents opportunities for AI to support the diagnostic process by identifying smaller but consistent differences. Various DL models analyzed otoscopic tympanic membrane images using convolutional neural networks (CNNs), a framework that extracts features like shapes and textures from raw pixel data to recognize objects. CNNs have been particularly effective in distinguishing between AOM, OME, and normal ears with an accuracy exceeding >90% [ 13 , 15 – 19 ]. Alternatively, recurrent neural networks (RNNs) are architectures that can “remember” information from previous inputs and makes predictions based on what it has seen before. Beyond static image analysis, RNNs have been applied to smartphone video-based otoscopy, to identify tympanic membrane features associated with acute otitis media ( Fig. 3 ) [ 20 ]. Sundgaard et al. (2022) used wideband tympanometry measurements for a CNN-driven algorithm to differentiate between AOM and OME [ 21 ]. These measurements provide a broader range of frequencies, translating into more model features. Similarly, Binol et al. (2020) successfully fused digital otoscopy videos with tympanometry data to improve the diagnosis of OME [ 22 ]. Figure 3. Deep learning framework for otitis media detection from otoscopic Images. Open in a new tab A comprehensive pipeline for automated ear disease diagnosis showing the workflow from otoscopy image acquisition through neural network processing (incorporating either Recurrent Neural Networks and Convolutional Neural Networks) to classification of tympanic membranes as either Normal or Otitis Media with Effusion (OME). The lower panel illustrates the automated features extracted by the network, identified from the ear images, with the final classification supporting clinical decision-making. DL models utilizing CNNs have been shown to achieve accuracies similar to well-experienced radiologists to assess adenoid size and its impact on airway obstruction, specifically with lateral cephalometry and plain neck films [ 23 , 24 ]. OSA, characterized by recurrent episodes of upper airway collapse during sleep, affects up to 5% of children and is associated with behavioral dysregulation and cognitive deficits [ 25 ]. Polysomnography is the standard for OSA diagnosis and severity stratification. However, it is costly, burdensome for children and not easily accessible [ 26 ]. To improve the OSA diagnostic process, several ML models have been employed to develop alternative diagnostic approaches using clinical features, non-invasive biosignals, and imaging data. Linear discriminant analysis, a classical machine learning method, can find linear combinations of features to classify OSA severity. Specifically, it uses non-nocturnal and non-medical patient data such as anatomical measurements, patient history, and other tools like the Pediatric Sleep Questionnaire [ 27 ]. Although the accuracies for these models ranged between 60–75%, this model is the first step for automated OSA diagnoses for children. Alternatively, Wang et al. (2021) developed a model to automate sleep stage classification from electroencephalography [ 28 ]. As sleep-stage analysis is the most labor-intensive aspect of polysomnography, automation can reduce the wait times. Levy et al. (2023) developed a CNN-based algorithm, OxiNet, utilizing single-channel oximetry data to predict OSA [ 29 ]. Although they primarily focused on adults with some pediatric data, OxiNet predicted >99% of moderate-to-severe OSA patients, compared to 21% for the best benchmark. Conversely, other studies have utilized polysomnography data to automate OSA diagnosis, differentiate between obstruction sites, and detect apneic events in real-time. A multi-layer perceptron model, which is a fully connected artificial neural network achieved high accuracy in diagnosing moderate-to-severe OSA. Importantly, this model used only single-channel nocturnal oxygen saturation data, without incorporating clinical variables [ 30 ]. Crowson et al. (2023) applied a CNN-based model to nasal pressure measurements obtained during polysomnography to identify specific breathing events to predict the the site of obstruction [ 31 ]. Lastly, algorithms have been developed to predict adenoid hypertrophy from nasopharyngoscopy images with polysomnography severity [ 32 ]. 3.2. Procedural Assistance DL algorithms can facilitate preoperative planning, intraoperative support, and surgical decision making. Endoscopic surgery is suitable for computer vision and CNN research [ 33 ]. Otologic surgery requires accurate identification of anatomical landmarks in a small space. Prior studies have investigated the use of automatic segmentation of clinical images for landmark identification. For instance, Mask R-CNN model can detect and segment the internal auditory canal [ 34 ] with Dice similarity coefficients in the 90s. In the surgical setting, Crowson et al. (2021) trained a ML model designed to predict the presence of OME based on the appearance of the tympanic membrane during tympanostomy tube placement [ 35 ]. This solution is particularly valuable for challenges clinicians face in visually assessing the tympanic membrane for effusion in the outpatient setting. Additionally, this tool aims to improve intraoperative decision-making, ensuring that effusions are accurately identified and appropriately managed in real-time. 3.3. Telemedicine and Remote Care The integration of AI into remote monitoring has the potential to detect conditions earlier, improve accessibility to care [ 36 ]. Tools leveraging DL can facilitate remote diagnosis for common conditions such as OME and OSA. AI-powered smartphone otoscopes detect OME based on CNN algorithm via images from a smartphone-enabled otoscope, which could avoid the need for an in-person evaluation [ 37 ]. This home-based application has the potential to reduce unnecessary clinic visits and expedite treatment. Similarly, a study developed a tele-otoscope equipped with Wi-Fi transmission, which is coupled with a cloud-based automatic OME diagnostic algorithm [ 38 ]. In addition to OME monitoring, DL models can monitor tympanostomy tubes over time, enabling parents to use a smartphone at home to check tympanostomy tube position [ 39 ]. 3.4. Patient Education Large language models (LLMs), like ChatGPT, are advanced AI systems that can understand and generate human-like text. These models have shown promise in several areas relevant to daily practice: streamlining clinical documentation, providing rapid literature summaries for clinical questions, and generating customized patient education materials [ 40 , 41 ]. AI-generated educational content needs to be assessed for ts accuracy, readability, and potential as a supplement to traditional physician counseling. Jongbloed and Grover (2024) evaluated ChatGPT’s responses to common questions and compared them with existing online resources. The study found that ChatGPT often provided responses with equal validity, and higher readability and comprehensiveness than many traditional websites, though intermittent inaccuracies highlighted the need for human oversight [ 42 ]. Similarly, another study investigated ChatGPT’s ability to simplify patient education materials. The findings suggested that AI could enhance accessibility by tailoring information to different literacy levels [ 43 ]. Beyond general information retrieval, AI has potential to be a virtual parent advisor for common procedures like tonsillectomies and tympanostomy tube insertions [ 44 , 45 ]. These studies have tested whether ChatGPT could provide parents with accurate and practical guidance about these surgeries. While AI-generated responses were generally informative, they lacked the individualized recommendations that physicians provide. These findings underscore ChatGPT’s potential as an adjunctive tool for patient education while reinforcing the necessity of clinician oversight in ensuring context-specific medical advice. 4. Future Directions Given the applications described above, AI’s full potential remains largely untapped. Emerging technological advancements combined with increasing availability of clinical data present new opportunities to enhance clinical decision-making and patient care. The following sections explore key areas where AI can further revolutionize the field, shaping more personalized, equitable, and effective care for children. In this section, we discuss the key areas where AI can further revolutionize the field, shaping more personalized and effective care for pediatric populations. 4.1. Developmental Trajectory Modeling in Clinical Practice Developmental trajectory models from temporally oriented data can identify subtle patterns and trajectories that elude traditional clinical observation. Prior research indicates that these frameworks can be particularly valuable in tracking the progression of chronic conditions such as hearing loss, vestibular disorders, and laryngeal pathologies [ 18 , 19 , 46 ]. The integration of multiple data streams, including audiometric measurements, imaging studies, and clinical observations, allows for more comprehensive prediction models that account for the complex interplay of various physiological parameters. 4.2. Collaborative Platforms and Data Sharing Initiatives Federated learning offers a practical solution to developing robust clinical AI systems while maintaining patient privacy and data security [ 47 ]. This approach allows hospitals to collaborate on AI development without sharing identifiable patient data, a crucial consideration for maintaining patient privacy. Where individual institutions might see fewer cases of congenital malformations or unusual airway masses, federated learning facilitates the development of AI diagnostic tools trained using data from multiple centers while keeping sensitive patient information secure. Research in other clinical domains such as radiology [ 48 , 49 ] and pathology [ 50 ] has highlighted practical considerations for departments participating in federated learning initiatives. Radiology federated learning deals with standardized image protocols, labeling conventions, and metadata to enable multiple sites to share imaging data without compromising patient privacy. In pathology, a similar approach is taken for digitized whole-slide images, ensuring consistent documentation of staining processes, slide scanning resolutions, and annotations across institutions. Different institutions often use varying terminology and documentation practices, requiring standardization before meaningful analysis can occur. Laryngology studies, for instance, have benefited from the establishment of standard protocols for voice recording and analysis across participating sites. 4.3. Behavioral AI and Psychosocial Integration The incorporation of psychosocial factors into AI models represents a critical evolution in making AI models more unbiased and accurate. Traditional AI systems have primarily focused on physiological and anatomical parameters without considering the importance of incorporating behavioral and psychological factors [ 51 , 52 ]. By analyzing natural language data from clinical notes, patient-reported outcomes, and social determinants of health, more nuanced models could predict patient outcomes better. 4.4. Large Language Models in Clinical Practice In the clinic, LLMs can assist with tasks such as automatically generating initial draft notes from patient encounters, summarizing complex cases for tumor boards, and providing quick references to current treatment guidelines. This capability has proven particularly valuable during busy clinic sessions where rapid access to information can enhance decision-making efficiency. However, clinicians must be aware of limitations. Most critically, these models can produce “hallucinations,” which are convincingly written but incorrect information that could impact patient care if not properly verified. These limitations necessitate verification of all LLM outputs by clinicians before clinical application. This multi-step verification process is essential in maintaining the safety and quality of patient care while leveraging the benefits of LLM technology. The implementation of LLMs in clinical practice requires several critical safeguards. Real-time fact-checking against current medical guidelines is a fundamental safety measure, while uncertainty quantification methods help clinicians assess the reliability of model outputs. By integrating domain-specific knowledge constraints we see significant improvements in accuracy of recommendations. Regular performance audits under specialist oversight ensure ongoing system reliability, while comprehensive documentation of model limitations helps establish appropriate use cases in clinical settings. 4.5. Algorithmic Bias Algorithmic bias represents a significant challenge in generalizing AI performance. The bias in clinical AI systems can exacerbate inequities. Understanding and addressing these biases has become a central consideration in the development and deployment of AI tools for clinical practice. The sources of algorithmic bias are multifaceted and subtle [ 53 ]. Training data may underrepresent certain demographic groups, reducing model performance for these populations. This challenge is particularly evident in areas such as voice analysis, where existing datasets often lack sufficient diversity in terms of age, gender, dialect, and cultural variation. Similar concerns arise in facial analysis algorithms, where training data may not adequately represent the full spectrum of demographic variations in facial features and structures. Clinical validation studies have revealed several critical areas where bias can impact patient care. AI systems trained predominantly on adult populations may not generalize to pediatric pathology [ 54 ]. Voice recognition algorithms may demonstrate varying performance across different accents and languages. Imaging analysis tools might show degraded performance across different skin tones or facial structures. Mitigation strategies for algorithmic bias require a comprehensive approach integrating technical solutions with clinical oversight. Systematic data collection efforts must prioritize demographic representation across age groups, ethnicities, and anatomical variations. This includes targeted data collection from minority populations and the development of specialized datasets that reflect the full spectrum of anatomical and physiological variation encountered in clinical practice. Regular audits of model performance across different demographic groups help refine algorithmic development. The implementation of bias detection and correction mechanisms has evolved to include both pre-deployment testing and ongoing monitoring. Advanced statistical techniques help quantify performance differences across population subgroups, while adaptive algorithms can adjust for identified biases in real-time. Clinical validation protocols increasingly emphasize testing across diverse populations, ensuring that performance metrics reflect real-world clinical scenarios. Documentation and transparency regarding potential biases have become essential components of AI deployment. Clinical teams must be aware of limitations or performance variations across different patient groups, enabling appropriate adjustment of clinical protocols and decision-making processes. This transparency extends to patient communication, where understanding potential limitations helps inform shared decision-making. Future directions in addressing algorithmic bias include the development of more sophisticated bias detection methods, improved techniques for dataset balancing, and the integration of fairness constraints into model training processes. Collaborative efforts across institutions to share diverse datasets while maintaining patient privacy will play a crucial role in developing more equitable AI systems. The goal remains the creation of AI tools that provide consistent, high-quality support for clinical decision-making across all patient populations. 4.6. Interpretability and Model Transparency Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) is a fundamental tool for making AI decisions transparent and interpretable. LIME creates simplified, interpretable models that approximate the behavior of complex AI systems for individual predictions. This localized approach proves particularly valuable in clinical settings where understanding the logic and rationale in diagnosis and management can improve patient care. In practical terms, LIME generates explanations by perturbing the input data and observing how the model’s predictions change [ 11 ]. For instance, in the analysis of laryngoscopy images, LIME might slightly modify different image regions to determine which areas most strongly influence AI-based diagnosis. The resulting explanation typically highlights specific regions or features that supported the model’s decision, presented in a way that aligns with clinical reasoning patterns. The proposed diagnostic pipeline ( Fig. 4 ) enables clinicians to understand the relative importance of different symptoms through LIME explanations before making final clinical decisions. Figure 4. Explainable AI framework using Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) for clinical decision support. Open in a new tab A schematic representation of an end-to-end pipeline for AI-assisted medical diagnosis. Patient data (e.g., demographics, symptoms) could be processed through a neural network model, followed by a Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) layer that generates feature importance metrics and visualizations. This enables clinicians to understand the AI’s reasoning before making final diagnostic decisions, enhancing transparency in medical AI applications. The model-agnostic nature of LIME enables the explanation of predictions from any AI model can provide essential flexibility across various clinical tasks. The technique’s focus on local fidelity supports individual predictions rather than attempting to explain entire model behavior, aligning well with clinical practice where understanding specific patient cases takes precedence. Furthermore, LIME transforms complex features into simpler, clinically meaningful representations, such as identifying frequency ranges in voice analysis or acoustic parameters that influence AI assessments, presenting them in terms familiar to clinicians. While LIME offers valuable insights, certain limitations warrant consideration in clinical applications. The technique may occasionally produce unstable explanations for complex cases, requiring validation. Additionally, the computational overhead of generating explanations must be managed to maintain clinical workflow efficiency. These limitations support clinical oversight. 5. Conclusions Artificial Intelligence is poised to support pediatric otolaryngology by offering more precise diagnostics, individualized treatment strategies, and expanded opportunities for remote patient monitoring. The application of deep learning methods has already enhanced the detection of common pediatric conditions such as OME and OSA with early clinical evaluations suggesting improved accuracy and efficiency. Yet, the path to widespread clinical integration remains contingent on mitigation of data shortage, ensuring robust model interpretability, and addressing algorithmic biases. Emerging technologies such as federated learning and longitudinal predictive modeling offer promising approaches to map disease progression patterns and personalize treatment. LLMs may streamline clinical documentation and patient education, provided they are deployed with rigorous oversight. Incorporating psychosocial dimensions into AI-driven evaluations further underscores the promise of holistic approaches. Cross-disciplinary collaborations and regulatory guidelines will be essential to fortify ethical, transparent, and reproducible AI systems. Together, these measures ensure that the transformative promise of AI yields tangible, long-term benefits for children and their families. Funding: Research reported in this manuscript was supported by National Heart Lung and Blood Institute of the National Institutes of Health under award number R01HL167012 to Amal Isaiah. Footnotes Conflicts of interest: None Data Statement: No relevant data for this publication. References [1]. Xu Y, Liu X, Cao X, Huang C, Liu E, Qian S, Liu X, Wu Y, Dong F, Qiu C-W, Qiu J, Hua K, Su W, Wu J, Xu H, Han Y, Fu C, Yin Z, Liu M, Roepman R, Dietmann S, Virta M, Kengara F, Zhang Z, Zhang L, Zhao T, Dai J, Yang J, Lan L, Luo M, Liu Z, An T, Zhang B, He X, Cong S, Liu X, Zhang W, Lewis JP, Tiedje JM, Wang Q, An Z, Wang F, Zhang L, Huang T, Lu C, Cai Z, Wang F, Zhang J, Artificial intelligence: A powerful paradigm for scientific research, The Innovation 2 (2021) 100179. 10.1016/j.xinn.2021.100179. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. LeCun Y, Bengio Y, Hinton G, Deep learning, Nature 521 (2015) 436–444. 10.1038/nature14539. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Topol EJ, High-performance medicine: the convergence of human and artificial intelligence, Nat. Med. 25 (2019) 44–56. 10.1038/s41591-018-0300-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Crowson MG, Ranisau J, Eskander A, Babier A, Xu B, Kahmke RR, Chen JM, Cha