2020) 153–169. 10.1016/j.ajhg.2019.12.010. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [21]. Knowles MR, Ostrowski LE, Leigh MW, Sears PR, Davis SD, Wolf WE, Hazucha MJ, Carson JL, Olivier KN, Sagel SD, Rosenfeld M, Ferkol TW, Dell SD, Milla CE, Randell SH, Yin W, Sannuti A, Metjian HM, Noone PG, Noone PJ, Olson CA, V Patrone M, Dang H, Lee H-S, Hurd TW, Gee HY, Otto EA, Halbritter J, Kohl S, Kircher M, Krischer J, Bamshad MJ, Nickerson DA, Hildebrandt F, Shendure J, Zariwala MA, Mutations in RSPH1 cause primary ciliary dyskinesia with a unique clinical and ciliary phenotype., Am. J. Respir. Crit. Care Med 189 (2014) 707–717. 10.1164/rccm.201311-2047OC. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [22]. Olbrich H, Cremers C, Loges NT, Werner C, Nielsen KG, Marthin JK, Philipsen M, Wallmeier J, Pennekamp P, Menchen T, Edelbusch C, Dougherty GW, Schwartz O, Thiele H, Altmüller J, Rommelmann F, Omran H, Loss-of-function GAS8 mutations cause primary ciliary dyskinesia and disrupt the dexin-dynein regulatory complex., Am. J. Hum. Genet 97 (2015) 546–554. 10.1016/j.ajhg.2015.08.012. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [23]. Edelbusch C, Cindrić S, Dougherty GW, Loges NT, Olbrich H, Rivlin J, Wallmeier J, Pennekamp P, Amirav I, Omran H, Mutation of serine/threonine protein kinase 36 (STK36) causes primary ciliary dyskinesia with a central pair defect., Hum. Mutat 38 (2017) 964–969. 10.1002/humu.23261. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [24]. Bustamante-Marin XM, Shapiro A, Sears PR, Charng W-L, Conrad DF, Leigh MW, Knowles MR, Ostrowski LE, Zariwala MA, Identification of genetic variants in CFAP221 as a cause of primary ciliary dyskinesia., J. Hum. Genet 65 (2020) 175–180. 10.1038/s10038-019-0686-1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [25]. Chivukula RR, Montoro DT, Leung HM, Yang J, Shamseldin HE, Taylor MS, Dougherty GW, Zariwala MA, Carson J, Daniels MLA, Sears PR, Black KE, Hariri LP, Almogarri I, Frenkel EM, Vinarsky V, Omran H, Knowles MR, Tearney GJ, Alkuraya FS, Sabatini DM, Author Correction: A human ciliopathy reveals essential functions for NEK10 in airway mucociliary clearance., Nat. Med 26 (2020) 300. 10.1038/s41591-020-0773-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',68,'2025-10-14 13:12:31','2025-10-14 13:12:31');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3055,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7135734','Distance learning and telemedicine in the area of Otorhinolaryngology: lessons in times of pandemic.','Machado Júnior AJ, Pauna HF.','2020','quarantines quarantines bans webcam admittedly transmitter pauna parmet e28 nejmp2004211 alunos enfermagem estao voluntariando combate sthisfb nejmp2003539 caieiros distancia diversidade ensino aberta contributos distancia tarpada gibber e219 e224','As COVID-19 is identified worldwide, governments have imposed quarantines and travel restrictions on an unprecedented scale. Countries such as China, Italy, Spain and the United States have imposed restrictions on their citizens or immigrants. Still, the number of cases and deaths continues to increase. 1 Quarantines and travel bans are often the first response to new infectious diseases. However, these old tools are usually of limited use for highly communicable diseases. 1 Currently, the discussion about the COVID-19 pandemic imposes restrictions on the mobilization of students (including in the health area), with classes being canceled and the teaching hospitals operating with “on duty” shifts only. 2 Flattening the curve of the number of new cases–slowing the spread of COVID-19 through space and time–is crucial. The health system cannot sustain a massive influx of infectious cases into emergency units and hospitals. Patients with mild symptoms should stay home whenever possible. Recognizing that many patients need guidance or recommendations, the use of virtual guidance services that have already been implemented or are under development can be an alternative to face-to-face visits to hospitals or medical offices. 3 A strategy that is starting to be implemented is telemedicine to the consumer, an approach that helps with efficient direct screening, helping to maintain the quarantine and protecting doctors and patients. Telemedicine allows doctors and patients to communicate using smartphones or webcam-enabled computers. Respiratory symptoms–admittedly COVID-19–are among the conditions most commonly assessed through this approach. Many medical decisions are cognitive ones, and telemedicine can provide quick access to other medical specialties that are not immediately available in person. Moreover, healthcare providers can easily obtain detailed travel and exposure histories and use algorithms to standardize guidance and information. Similarly, distance learning (DL) is a method that has been widely used as a tool in helping to disseminate culture and knowledge. Many countries have benefited from this tool, and in Brazil, a country of continental dimensions, this modality has been shown to be an effective method of spreading knowledge, considering the DL figures throughout the country. 4 A recurring discussion in the mass and specialized media is the use of distance learning in the health area. Professional class entities in the health area have questioned and even opposed the use of DL as a training method in undergraduate courses carried out entirely at distance, although other areas are benefiting from this tool. There is a wide range of tools used in DL among the researched articles: they range from online tools, use of tablets and applications for smartphones, to 3D-anatomical pieces. However, a condition is recurrent in these studies: these tools are used as a complement in the teaching-learning process and do not take away the teacher''s role in this relationship. 5 The successful use of DL in medical education requires it to meet the needs of students and it must be aligned with the program and the contexts in which it is used. Researchers have noted that individual differences can also play an important role in the effectiveness of these tools. Students preferred e-learning technologies and perform better with digital media systems, because they use active approaches and make better transfer of concepts in new situations. 4 Although several studies point to new paths regarding the use of virtual tools for patient guidance or teaching-learning, the limits to which the medical content should be made available are not known. Currently, in Brazil, there is a great discussion about the non-use of DL in undergraduate courses in the health area; however, among the reviewed articles, more than half show successful DL experiences in undergraduate medical courses (and also in the area of otorhinolaryngology), not only in theoretical disciplines, but also in practical and surgical ones. These studies use DL as a complementary tool in the teaching-learning process. However, how many hours and how much content should be transmitted in this modality? Should we rethink the teaching-learning dynamic? Will the teacher''s role as a transmitter of knowledge be limited to the advent of distance learning? New studies must be carried out to evaluate these variables. Disasters and pandemics represent unique challenges to education and health care services. Although telemedicine does not solve all of them, it is adequate for scenarios where the infrastructure remains intact and doctors are available to assist patients. Payment and regulatory structures, state licensing, hospital accreditation and program implementation take time to be completed, but health systems that have already invested in telemedicine are well established to ensure that patients with COVID-19 receive the care they need. Conflicts of interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Footnotes ☆ Please cite this article as: Machado Júnior AJ, Pauna HF. Distance learning and telemedicine in the area of Otorhinolaryngology: lessons in times of pandemic. Braz J Otorhinolaryngol. 2020;86:271–2. References 1. Parmet W.E., Sinha M.S. COVID-19 – the law and limits of quarantine. N Engl J Med. 2020;382:e28. doi: 10.1056/NEJMp2004211. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Mori L. 2020. Coronavirus: Os alunos de medicina e enfermagem que estão se voluntariando no combate ao COVID-19. https://www.bbc.com/portuguese/brasil-52043644?SThisFB [accessed 29.3.20] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Hollander J.E., Carr B.G. Virtually perfect? Telemedicine for COVID-19. N Engl J Med. 2020;382:1679–1681. doi: 10.1056/NEJMp2003539. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Caieiros D. In: Educação a distância e diversidade no ensino superior. Aires L., et al., editors. Universidade Aberta; Lisboa: 2014. Contributos para uma estratégia nacional em educação a distância e e-learning; pp. 9–18. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Tarpada S.P., Hsueh W.D., Gibber M.J. Resident and student education in otolaryngology: a 10-year update on e-learning: E-learning in otolaryngology education. Laryngoscope. 2017;127:E219–E224. doi: 10.1002/lary.26320. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',180,'2025-10-14 13:12:43','2025-10-14 13:12:43');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3056,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608682','Evaluating a specialized workshop on otorhinolaryngology emergencies for junior doctors: Empowering the next generation of healers.','Giri S, Khan SA, Parajuli SB, Rauniyar Z, Rimal A.','2024','nonparticipation irc birat crafted nearing embarked underprepared rehearse jeopardizing solidify posttests inflating sandarva giri sandarva giri zenith rauniyar anurag rimal sandarva giri sajjad sandarva giri sajjad surya bahadur parajuli giri parajuli rauniyar rimal e40771 sandarva giri sandervgiri surya bahadur parajuli splochan695 zenith rauniyar drsajjadahmedkhan anurag rimal rugginator101 whitcroft mcrae mehtani tolley bootcamp schaerer mafee crotty sahoo lukama aldous michelo kalinda e0281686 malekzadeh malloy tompkins battista onboard larraga moslehi masoumi barghi shirazi kinsman radomski heining amlani doshi','Abstract Otorhinolaryngology (ORL) emergencies, including airway obstructions, epistaxis, and foreign body removal, require prompt and specialized medical intervention. Junior doctors, particularly those in the early stages of their careers, often face challenges in managing these conditions due to limited exposure to ORL-specific training during their internships. While general medical education provides foundational skills, it may not sufficiently prepare junior doctors to handle the complexities of ORL emergencies. As junior doctors are often the first point of contact for urgent ORL cases in emergency departments, targeted training is critical to enhance their competence and improve patient outcomes. This study assessed the effectiveness of a specialized workshop focused on ORL emergencies, attended by 41 junior doctors. The cohort had varying levels of clinical experience, with 31 having completed 6 months and 10 having completed 12 months of internship. Of the participants, 25 had prior exposure to ORL during their internship rotations, while 16 had not. The cohort had a mean age of 24.73 years (SD = ±1.30), with a gender distribution of 21 females and 20 males. None of the participants had previously attended formal training specifically focused on ORL emergencies. Knowledge improvement was measured using pre- and posttest scores, with statistical analysis to assess the significance of the change. The mean pretest score was 9.95 (SD = 2.97), and the mean posttest score was 12.78 (SD = 2.73). The improvement in scores was statistically significant ( P < .0001), indicating that the workshop was effective in enhancing participants’ knowledge of ORL emergencies. The workshop significantly improved junior doctors’ knowledge of ORL emergencies, addressing important gaps in their training. By providing specialized education to a diverse group of early-career doctors, the initiative enhanced their preparedness to manage urgent ORL conditions, ultimately contributing to better patient care and safety in emergency settings. Keywords: emergencies, junior doctor, knowledge, Otorhinolaryngology, skills 1. Introduction Otorhinolaryngology (ORL) emergencies encompass a wide variety of acute conditions that often require prompt medical intervention to prevent life-threatening complications. These include airway obstruction, epistaxis, foreign body aspiration, and severe laryngeal trauma. Such emergencies are time-sensitive, requiring rapid diagnosis and intervention to avoid significant morbidity or mortality. Junior doctors, particularly those in the early stages of their careers, often serve as the first point of contact for patients presenting with these urgent ORL conditions in emergency departments or other acute care settings. However, they may not always be adequately prepared due to limited exposure to otorhinolaryngology-specific training during their medical education and internship rotations. [ 1 , 2 ] While general medical education provides foundational knowledge, it often lacks the depth needed to manage complex and life-threatening ORL conditions, leaving many junior doctors feeling unprepared and lacking confidence. [ 3 ] This gap in training can lead to delays in diagnosis, suboptimal management, and poor patient outcomes. [ 4 , 5 ] Recent studies have highlighted the need for enhanced training in ORL emergencies for junior doctors, particularly in high-risk areas such as airway management and bleeding control. [ 6 , 7 ] Research indicates that when junior doctors are exposed to focused training programs–such as simulation-based learning, workshops, and hands-on clinical training–they experience significant improvements in both knowledge and confidence. [ 8 , 9 ] Moreover, such interventions have been shown to improve clinical performance, leading to more effective management of urgent conditions and a reduction in errors. [ 10 ] This underscores the growing recognition that specialized education in ORL emergencies is critical for improving both patient care and the safety of junior doctors as they navigate the challenges of their early medical careers. Given these findings, there has been increasing emphasis on incorporating focused educational initiatives, such as workshops dedicated to ORL emergencies, into the training of junior doctors. These initiatives aim to provide them with the necessary skills to confidently manage common but critical ORL conditions such as airway obstruction, severe epistaxis, and laryngeal trauma, all of which require immediate intervention to prevent life-threatening complications. [ 11 , 12 ] The present study seeks to evaluate the effectiveness of such a specialized workshop, designed to fill the gap in training and enhance the preparedness of junior doctors to handle ORL emergencies effectively. By measuring improvements in knowledge and confidence, this research aims to assess whether targeted interventions can positively influence the readiness of junior doctors in managing high-acuity ORL cases. 2. Methods 2.1. Study participants and selection criteria The study targeted junior doctors who had recently completed their Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery (MBBS) final examinations and were undergoing a mandatory 1-year internship rotation. These participants were selected based on their registration for a specialized training program focusing on Essential otorhinolaryngology Emergencies, organized by the Department of otorhinolaryngology. This cohort represented a diverse group of medical professionals eager to enhance their skills in managing critical ENT conditions. 2.2. Inclusion criteria Participants were required to have successfully completed their MBBS final examinations and actively registered for the 1-day training session on otorhinolaryngology emergencies. This ensured that all participants possessed the foundational medical knowledge necessary to engage meaningfully with the training content. 2.3. Exclusion criteria Exclusion criteria encompassed individuals who did not complete their MBBS program or who did not formally register for the training program. Nonparticipation in the scheduled sessions also excluded interns from being included in the final analysis, ensuring data integrity and the effectiveness of the training evaluation. 2.4. Study duration and enrollment The training program, essential for advancing knowledge and practical skills in otorhinolaryngology emergencies, was meticulously designed to span a single intensive day. Initially, 50 interns demonstrated interest and registered for the program, highlighting the relevance and appeal of specialized training opportunities among early-career medical professionals. Ultimately, 41 interns actively participated in the structured training sessions, indicating a robust engagement and commitment to professional development. 2.5. Sample size calculation Given the comprehensive nature of the study and the invitation extended to all eligible interns, a formal sample size calculation was not necessary. The inclusion of all interested and qualified participants ensured a representative sample and minimized selection bias, reflecting the real-world context of medical education and training. 2.6. Ethical considerations Ethical considerations were paramount throughout the study process. Ethical clearance was taken from institutional review committee (IRC) of Birat Medical College Teaching Hospital. Further, informed consent was obtained from all participants, detailing the study objectives, procedures, potential risks, and benefits associated with participation. Strict protocols were in place to safeguard participant anonymity and confidentiality, protecting their identities and responses throughout data collection and analysis. Participants were assured that their involvement in the study would not result in any adverse consequences or impacts on their internship evaluations or professional standing. 2.7. Data collection and analysis Data collection commenced with the administration of a pretest questionnaire distributed via Google Forms, comprising 20 carefully crafted multiple-choice questions. The questionnaire covered a broad spectrum of otorhinolaryngology emergencies, including ear emergencies, vertigo management, tracheostomy indications, epistaxis, paranasal sinus emergencies, pharynx and neck space emergencies, anaphylaxis, drug-related emergencies, and traumatic ENT emergencies. Participants were allotted a defined time limit of 10 minutes to complete the questionnaire, ensuring consistency and efficiency in data gathering. 2.8. Statistical analysis Following the intensive training sessions, a post-training assessment using the identical 20-question format was administered to evaluate participants’ knowledge retention and performance improvement. Scores from both pretest and posttest assessments, along with the corresponding answer key, were promptly communicated to participants via email for transparency and self-assessment. The collected data from pre- and post-assessment questionnaires were meticulously compiled using Google Sheets and subjected to rigorous statistical analysis using SPSS 23 software. Descriptive statistics, including means, standard deviations, and frequency distributions, provided a comprehensive summary of participants’ scores. Inferential statistical techniques, such as paired t -tests, were employed to compare pretest and posttest scores, elucidating the statistical significance of learning outcomes and the program’s effectiveness in enhancing participants’ competency in managing critical ENT emergencies. 3. Results The study cohort comprised 41 junior doctors who actively participated in a specialized workshop focused on Essential otorhinolaryngology Emergencies, organized by the Department of otorhinolaryngology. The mean age of the participants was 24.73 years, with a standard deviation of ±1.30 years, indicating a relatively narrow age distribution among the attendees, reflective of the cohort’s early-career stage in medicine. Gender distribution among the participants was nearly balanced, with 21 female and 20 male doctors attending the workshop. This gender parity reflects the growing diversity within the medical profession and underscores the inclusive nature of the training initiative. In terms of internship experience, 31 candidates had completed 6 months of their mandatory internship period, while 10 candidates had completed the full 12-month internship requirement. This variation in internship duration provided a spectrum of clinical exposure among the participants, with some having completed a significant portion of their internship training and others nearing its completion. Regarding clinical exposure specifically related to otorhinolaryngology specialties, 25 candidates had already undergone an otorhinolaryngology posting as part of their internship rotations. This subgroup had prior exposure to otorhinolaryngology -related conditions and procedures, potentially influencing their baseline knowledge and readiness to engage with the workshop content. In contrast, 16 candidates had not yet embarked on an otorhinolaryngology posting, indicating a lack of prior specialized training in otorhinolaryngology emergencies prior to the workshop (Table 1 ). Similarly, the mean and standard deviation of pretest score and posttest score were 9.95 and 2.97 and 12.78 and 2.73 respectively. It was found statistically significant ( P < .0001) (Table 2 ). Table 1. Baseline characteristics of the participants (N = 41). Parameters Category Number (N) Percentage (%) Age (yr) 23.0 5 12.2 24.0 15 36.6 25.0 14 34.1 26.0 3 7.3 27.0 2 4.9 28.0 1 2.4 29.0 1 2.4 Mean ± SD 24.73 ± 1.30 − Sex Male 20 48.8 Female 21 51.2 Duration of work as intern (mo) 6 31 75.6 12 10 24.4 Mean ± SD 7.463 ± 2.61 − Completed internship posting at department of ENT-HNS? Yes 25 61.0 No 16 39.0 Attended a previous seminar/webinar/workshop on ENT emergencies? Yes 0 0 No 41 100 Open in a new tab ENT = ear, nose and throat, HNS = head and neck surgery. Table 2. Pretest and posttest comparison of improvement of knowledge and skills of junior doctor attending essential emergencies in ENT workshop (N = 41). Parameters Number (N) Mean Standard deviation Standard error mean Confidence interval P value Pretest 41 9.95 2.97 0.463 −3.702 to −1.957 <.0001 * , † Posttest 41 12.78 2.73 0.427 Open in a new tab * Statistically significant. † Paired t test. An important aspect highlighted was the participants’ lack of prior attendance at seminars, webinars, or workshops specifically focused on otorhinolaryngology emergencies. None of the participants had previously engaged in formalized educational sessions dedicated to otorhinolaryngology emergencies, emphasizing the workshop’s novelty and the unique educational opportunity it provided for enhancing participants’ knowledge and skills in this critical area of medical practice. Overall, the demographic and training background of the participants underscored the workshop’s relevance and potential impact in filling educational gaps and equipping junior doctors with essential competencies for managing otorhinolaryngology emergencies effectively. By targeting a diverse group of early-career doctors with varying levels of clinical exposure and prior otorhinolaryngology training, the workshop aimed to standardize and elevate the participants’ proficiency in handling urgent otorhinolaryngology conditions, thereby enhancing overall patient care and safety in emergency settings. 4. Discussion The management of otorhinolaryngology (ORL) emergencies is a critical aspect of acute medical care. Early-career doctors, particularly those in internship or junior doctor roles, are often the first responders to these emergencies in emergency departments or general hospital settings. However, as demonstrated by this study and supported by previous research, junior doctors often lack the specific training and preparedness required to manage such cases effectively. The results of this study underscore the value of targeted educational interventions, such as specialized workshops, in improving knowledge, skills, and confidence in handling ORL emergencies. The findings from this study corroborate those of previous research, which has highlighted significant gaps in the training of junior doctors regarding ORL emergencies. A large proportion of the participants in this study had limited or no prior exposure to ORL-specific emergencies, with many not having participated in any formal ORL-focused training programs before attending the workshop. This lack of specialized training is consistent with the findings of Nair et al, who noted that junior doctors often report feeling underprepared for ORL emergencies due to insufficient exposure during their medical education and clinical rotations. [ 2 ] In many medical schools, the focus of undergraduate training remains on general medicine and surgery, with specialties like otorhinolaryngology receiving less emphasis, which can contribute to significant knowledge gaps. [ 1 ] These gaps are particularly concerning given that ORL emergencies often require rapid and specialized interventions, such as airway management in cases of laryngeal obstruction or the control of severe epistaxis, where delays in treatment can lead to life-threatening outcomes. [ 3 , 4 ] The results from this study indicate a significant improvement in both the theoretical knowledge and practical confidence of junior doctors following the ORL emergency workshop. The pre- and posttest scores showed a marked increase, suggesting that the targeted training had a positive impact on participants’ understanding of ORL emergency management. These findings are in line with those of previous studies, revealing that focused educational workshops for junior doctors in ORL significantly enhanced their clinical knowledge and skills in managing emergencies. [ 7 ] Workshops like the 1 described in this study are valuable not only in providing theoretical knowledge but also in offering hands-on experience and practical training. Participants who engage in such training are often more confident and capable when faced with real-life emergencies, as evidenced by the improvements in their post-workshop self-assessment and performance. [ 6 ] Simulation-based training has been particularly effective in teaching skills for emergency care, as it allows junior doctors to practice in a low-risk environment. Simulation-based workshops provide a platform for learners to rehearse emergency procedures and decision-making processes without jeopardizing patient safety. [ 8 ] This approach enhances the ability of junior doctors to respond effectively to high-stress situations in actual clinical settings. Simulation also promotes active learning, where participants can interact with equipment, practice key procedures such as intubation or nasal packing, and refine their decision-making abilities under supervision. The structured feedback provided during these sessions further reinforces learning and skill development. [ 5 ] One of the most crucial aspects of ORL emergency care is airway management. Airway obstruction, whether caused by foreign body aspiration, laryngeal trauma, or swelling, is a potentially life-threatening emergency that requires prompt and decisive action. The ability to quickly assess and secure the airway is a core competency that all junior doctors should possess, particularly when they are often the first healthcare professionals to encounter these cases in emergency settings. [ 9 ] The workshop described in this study specifically focused on airway management techniques, including the use of advanced airway devices, and helped participants practice these skills in a controlled, supervised environment. The importance of airway management training has been highlighted by several studies, that delays in managing airway obstruction are a significant cause of morbidity and mortality in emergency settings, particularly when junior doctors lack the experience or confidence to intervene promptly. [ 3 ] This study supports the idea that well-structured workshops can address this gap by equipping junior doctors with the necessary skills to manage airway emergencies efficiently. Through repeated practice and simulation, junior doctors are better prepared to make critical decisions when managing patients with compromised airways, ultimately improving patient outcomes. While the immediate improvements in knowledge and confidence are encouraging, the long-term impact of specialized workshops remains an important area for further research. Sustained improvements in clinical practice and confidence depend on how well new knowledge is integrated into everyday clinical settings. [ 9 ] Although junior doctors may show marked improvements immediately following training, it is essential to evaluate how well they retain and apply these skills over time. Continued support and reinforcement, such as through follow-up sessions or online educational modules, could help solidify the knowledge gained during workshops and ensure its practical application in real-world emergency settings. Future studies should aim to track the long-term outcomes of such interventions, including clinical decision-making, patient safety, and reduction in errors related to ORL emergencies. Moreover, the increasing demand for specialized training in ORL suggests that similar workshops could be integrated into routine medical curricula. The growing complexity of patient care in emergency departments, coupled with the increasing number of cases involving ORL conditions, makes it imperative that junior doctors receive comprehensive training in this area. Furthermore, expanding training opportunities to include multidisciplinary teams could further improve collaboration and enhance patient care during ORL emergencies. As noted by Brooks et al, structured emergency training not only improves the response of junior doctors but also facilitates better teamwork and communication within healthcare teams, leading to more coordinated and effective management of critical cases. [ 12 ] 5. Conclusions This study demonstrates that specialized workshops focused on ORL emergencies can significantly improve junior doctors’ knowledge, skills, and confidence in managing urgent ORL conditions. The results highlight the importance of targeted educational initiatives in bridging the training gaps that often exist in the early stages of medical careers. Given the life-threatening nature of many ORL emergencies, such as airway obstruction and severe epistaxis, providing junior doctors with the tools to manage these conditions is essential for improving patient outcomes. Future research should further explore the long-term effects of such training programs and evaluate their broader implementation in medical education. This study has several limitations. The relatively small sample size of 41 interns may limit generalizability, and the voluntary nature of participation could introduce selection bias, as attendees may have had a greater interest in ENT. Additionally, using the same questionnaire for both pre- and posttests raises concerns about familiarity bias, potentially inflating posttest scores. The lack of a control group restricts our ability to attribute knowledge gains solely to the workshop. Furthermore, while cognitive knowledge was assessed, the study did not evaluate practical skills in real or simulated scenarios. It also did not explore participants’ interests in procedural versus non-procedural specialties, which could impact engagement. Lastly, the focus on immediate outcomes without long-term follow-up and the absence of psychometric evaluation for the assessments raise questions about reliability and sustainability of the findings. Acknowledging these limitations is essential for interpreting the results and informing future research. Acknowledgments Authors are grateful to all the study participants for their co-operation and support. Author contributions Conceptualization: Sandarva Giri. Data curation: Sandarva Giri, Zenith Rauniyar, Anurag Rimal. Writing – original draft: Sandarva Giri, Sajjad Ahmed Khan. Writing – review & editing: Sandarva Giri, Sajjad Ahmed Khan, Surya Bahadur Parajuli. Abbreviation: ORL otorhinolaryngology The authors have no funding and conflicts of interest to disclose. The datasets generated during and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. How to cite this article: Giri S, Khan SA, Parajuli SB, Rauniyar Z, Rimal A. Evaluating a specialized workshop on otorhinolaryngology emergencies for junior doctors: Empowering the next generation of healers. Medicine 2024;103:48(e40771). Contributor Information Sandarva Giri, Email: sandervgiri@gmail.com. Surya Bahadur Parajuli, Email: splochan695@gmail.com. Zenith Rauniyar, Email: drsajjadahmedkhan@gmail.com. Anurag Rimal, Email: Rugginator101@gmail.com. References [1]. Whitcroft KL, Moss B, Mcrae A. ENT and airways in the emergency department: national survey of junior doctors’ knowledge and skills. J Laryngol Otol. 2016;130:183–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Rai A, Shukla S, Mehtani N, Acharya V, Tolley N. Does a junior doctor focused ‘Bootcamp’ improve the confidence and preparedness of newly appointed ENT registrars to perform their job roles? BMC Med Educ. 2024;24:702. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Joshua J, Scholten E, Schaerer D, Mafee MF, Alexander TH, Crotty Alexander LE. Otolaryngology in critical care. Ann Am Thorac Soc. 2018;15:643–54. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Sahoo GC. Emergencies in Otorhinolaryngology. JP Medical Ltd.; 2014. [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Lukama L, Aldous C, Michelo C, Kalinda C. Ear, nose and throat (ENT) disease diagnostic error in low-resource health care: observations from a hospital-based cross-sectional study. PLoS One. 2023;18:e0281686. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Wu KY, Kim S, Fung K, Roth K. Assessing nontechnical skills in otolaryngology emergencies through simulation-based training. Laryngoscope. 2018;128:2301–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [7]. Malekzadeh S, Malloy KM, Chu EE, Tompkins J, Battista A, Deutsch ES. ORL emergencies boot camp: using simulation to onboard residents. Laryngoscope. 2011;121:2114–21. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [8]. Larraga-García B, Quintana-Díaz M, Gutiérrez A. Simulation-based education in trauma management: a scoping review. Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2022;19:13546. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [9]. Moslehi S, Masoumi G, Barghi-Shirazi F. Benefits of simulation-based education in hospital emergency departments: a systematic review. J Educ Health Promot. 2022;11:40. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [10]. Gottlieb M, Long B. Managing epistaxis. Ann Emerg Med. 2023;81:234–40. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [11]. Callaghan A, Kinsman L, Cooper S, Radomski N. The factors that influence junior doctors’ capacity to recognise, respond and manage patient deterioration in an acute ward setting: an integrative review. Aust Crit Care. 2017;30:197–209. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [12]. Heining CJ, Amlani A, Doshi J. Ambulatory management of common ENT emergencies: what’s the evidence? J Laryngol Otol. 2021;135:191–5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',10,'2025-10-14 13:12:53','2025-10-14 13:12:53');
