operties of the Hearing Handicap Inventory for the Elderly. Ear Hear. 2020;41(5):1125‐1134. doi: 10.1097/AUD.0000000000000832 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 84. Molinengo G, Baiardini I, Braido F, Loera B. RhinAsthma patient perspective: a Rasch validation study. J Asthma. 2018;55(2):119‐123. doi: 10.1080/02770903.2017.1316391 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 85. Rodriguez AM, Frenkiel S, Desroches J, et al. Development and validation of the McGill body image concerns scale for use in head and neck oncology (MBIS‐HNC): a mixed‐methods approach. Psychooncology. 2019;28(1):116‐121. doi: 10.1002/pon.4918 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 86. Declau F, Pingnet L, Verkest V, Hansen T. Cross‐cultural evaluation of the Dutch FACE‐Q rhinoplasty questionnaires using Rasch analysis. Aesthet Surg J. 2021;41(12):NP1916‐NP1930. doi: 10.1093/asj/sjab217 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 87. McRackan TR, Hand BN, Chidarala S, Dubno JR. Understanding patient expectations before implantation using the Cochlear Implant Quality of Life‐Expectations instrument. JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2022;148(9):870‐878. doi: 10.1001/jamaoto.2022.2292 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 88. Graboyes EM, Hand BN, Ellis MA, et al. Validation of a novel, multidomain head and neck cancer appearance‐ and function‐distress patient‐reported outcome measure. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2020;163(5):979‐985. doi: 10.1177/0194599820927364 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 89. McRackan TR, Hand BN, Velozo CA, Dubno JR. Development of the cochlear implant quality of life item bank. Ear Hear. 2019;40(4):1016‐1024. doi: 10.1097/AUD.0000000000000684 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 90. McRackan TR, Hand BN, Velozo CA, Dubno JR. Cochlear Implant Quality of Life (CIQOL): development of a profile instrument (CIQOL‐35 Profile) and a global measure (CIQOL‐10 Global). J Speech Lang Hear Res. 2019;62(9):3554‐3563. doi: 10.1044/2019_JSLHR-H-19-0142 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials PRISMA 2020 Checklist . Click here for additional data file. (65.8KB, pdf)',277,'2025-10-14 13:22:02','2025-10-14 13:22:02');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3100,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7300878','Proactive measures for the pandemic COVID-19 infection in outpatient clinics of Otolaryngology Department.','Chien CY, Wu JY, Wang LF.','2020','hyposmia dysgeusia fiberscope sanitize tocc enquired tocc hypopharynx pledgets soaked decongestant fiberscope disinfected fiberscope jyk ewy juang kaohsiung swx kalimuddin','Dear Editor, The coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID‐19) has become a serious pandemic. According to a report from Wuhan, China, 40 (29%) health professionals were among the first 138 patients to be consecutively hospitalized. 1 The reported symptoms of COVID‐19 included cough (67.8%), fever (43.8%), increased sputum production (33.7%), sore throat (13.9%), and nasal congestion (4.8%). 2 Some patients had only hyposmia and dysgeusia as their initial symptoms. In particular, health care workers, such as otolaryngologists, who work on upper aerodigestive tract diseases, have the highest risk of contracting COVID‐19. Specifically, the first physicians to die from COVID‐19 in Wuhan and from severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in Hong Kong pandemic were otolaryngologists. 3 Infection prevention protocols should be modified to ensure that otolaryngologists can appropriately adhere to them in their daily practice. Ideally, nonurgent head and neck procedures, including operations, should be postponed to limit the transmission of COVID‐19 from infected patients to health care workers. However, patients suspected of having foreign body impaction or head and neck cancers still require fiberscope examination. In our hospital, everyone must wear surgical masks, sanitize their hands, and have their body temperature checked at a quarantine station. 4 Before entering our facility, all patients'' travel, occupation, contact, and cluster histories (TOCC) are enquired into and logged electronically. Those with a fever or with high‐risk TOCC profiles are not allowed to enter the hospital; they are referred to either an outdoor clinic or the Emergency Department. Endoscopic examinations of the nose, sinuses, nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx, and larynx are common diagnostic procedures, but they generate aerosols. A study noted that for 83% of patients in Singapore, viral shedding from the nasopharynx continued for 7 days or longer. 5 Therefore, examiners should wear personal protective equipment, which includes N95 mask, a powered air‐purifying respirator, tight‐fitting goggles, a face shield, a gown, a disposable cap, shoe covers, and a pair of gloves. Patients should be housed in private negative‐pressure wards. For alert patients, topical anesthesia should not be administered through a spray. Instead, we adopt a careful placement of pledgets soaked with decongestant and topical anesthetics in the patient''s nose before examination. This method avoids triggering the sneezing and coughing reflex in patients. Patients are asked to use a surgical mask to cover their mouth when we insert the fiberscope through their nose during the procedure. We prefer using a video monitoring system to prevent close contact with the patients. The other benefit of using video is that the examiner can stand at the side rather than the front of the patient, thus avoiding contact with the aerosol generated during the procedure. After examinations, the environment and all used equipment must be disinfected. It is mandatory to wash hands with soap and water or alcohol‐based hand rub before and after all patients'' interaction. We discuss fiberscope use in otolaryngology in the context of the COVID‐19 pandemic. We also discuss the COVID‐19 protection measures mandated by our Department of Otolaryngology; these measures protect the clinical workforce from COVID‐19 infection. CONFLICT OF INTEREST The authors declare no potential conflict of interest. REFERENCES 1. Wang D, Hu B, Hu C, Zhu F, Liu X, Zhang J , et al. Clinical characteristics of 138 hospitalized patients with 2019 novel coronavirus‐infected pneumonia in Wuhan, China. JAMA. 2020;323(11):1061–1069. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Guan WJ, Ni ZY, Hu Y, Liang WH, Ou CQ, He JX , et al. Clinical characteristics of coronavirus disease 2019 in China. N Engl J Med. 2020;382:1708–1720. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Chan JYK, Wong EWY, Lam W. Practical aspects of otolaryngologic clinical services during the 2019 novel coronavirus epidemic: An experience in Hong Kong. JAMA Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2020. 10.1001/jamaoto.2020.0488. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Juang SF, Chiang HC, Tsai MJ, Huang MK. Integrated hospital quarantine system against COVID‐19. Kaohsiung J Med Sci. 2020;36:380–381. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Young BE, Ong SWX, Kalimuddin S, Low JG, Tan SY, Loh J , et al. Epidemiologic features and clinical course of patients infected with SARS‐CoV‐2 in Singapore. JAMA. 2020;323:1488. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',11900,'2025-10-14 13:22:20','2025-10-14 13:22:20');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3101,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11232560','Admission practices following pediatric tonsillectomy: A survey of ASPO members.','Rollins JT, Wajsberg B, Bitners AC, Burton WB, Hametz PA, Chambers TA, Yang CJ.','2022','aspo hypopnea hypopnea concurring aspo aspo ents tabulations clarifications hypopnea polysomnogram hypopnea demographically adherers hypopnea commenters aspo nihms1951810 supp_fig_1 nihms1951810 supp_fig_2 nihms1951810 supp_fig_3 342kb nihms1951810 supp_fig_4 nihms1951810 supp_fig_5 nihms1951810 supp_fig_6 503kb nihms1951810 supp_fig_7 nihms1951810 supp_fig_8 nihms1951810 supp_fig_9 nihms1951810 supp_fig_10 nihms1951810 supp_fig_11 nihms1951810 supp_fig_12 nihms1951810 supp_fig_13 nihms1951810 supp_fig_14 nihms1951810 supp_fig_15 nihms1951810 supp_fig_16 nihms1951810 supp_fig_17 maximilian ctsa ul1tr001073 hypopnea aspo schwartzman fehrm nerfeldt browaldh watchful patino sadhasivam mahmoud i83 i95 kermack manoukian brouillette e19 kermack pulmonol bachenberg benumof litvak canapari koomson brouillette ishman s42 mccolley naclerio loughlin schwengel sterni tunkel heitmiller lakheeram morielli brouillette postadenotonsillectomy keamy chhabra hartnick bitners arens nardone jatana elmaraghy villavisanis richman kulbersh e389 e394 belyea rigby corsten spiceland ishman jcsm alsuhebani walia therapeut roberson shemanski farias e801 nihms1951810 supp_fig_1 nihms1951810 supp_fig_2 nihms1951810 supp_fig_3 342kb nihms1951810 supp_fig_4 nihms1951810 supp_fig_5 nihms1951810 supp_fig_6 503kb nihms1951810 supp_fig_7 nihms1951810 supp_fig_8 nihms1951810 supp_fig_9 nihms1951810 supp_fig_10 nihms1951810 supp_fig_11 nihms1951810 supp_fig_12 nihms1951810 supp_fig_13 nihms1951810 supp_fig_14 nihms1951810 supp_fig_15 nihms1951810 supp_fig_16 nihms1951810 supp_fig_17','Abstract Objectives: Although evidence-based Clinical Practice Guidelines (CPGs) have specified postoperative admission criteria for pediatric tonsillectomy, there is substantial variation in guideline implementation and adherence among otolaryngologists in practice. We aimed to assess pediatric otolaryngologists’ post-tonsillectomy admission practices and to examine patient and surgeon factors associated with differences in admission practices. Methods: An electronic cross-sectional survey was distributed to members of the American Society of Pediatric Otolaryngology (ASPO) to determine current practices regarding admission practices following pediatric tonsillectomy. Chi-square and Fisher’s exact tests were performed to compare differences in adherence to tonsillectomy CPGs by respondent characteristics. Results: The survey was sent to 644 pediatric otolaryngologists with a response rate of 19.1%. 37% of respondents reported “always” and 60% “often” using the Academy of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery (AAO-HNS) CPG to guide decision for admission. Years in practice was the factor most strongly associated with admission practices, with 10 or fewer years in practice significantly correlated with stricter adherence to the AAO-HNS CPG of overnight observation when Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI) ≥10, age <3 years, or O2 nadir <80%) (OR 4.2, p <0.001), as well as specific individual criteria such as an AHI ≥10 (OR 4.1, p = 0.03). Respondents in an academic practice setting were more likely to admit children <3 years of age than those in private practice (OR 5.0, p = 0.01). Conclusion: Admission practices varied among pediatric otolaryngologist survey respondents, and strict AAO- HNS CPG adherence was associated with fewer years in practice and academic practice setting. These results suggest that further study investigating factors influencing guideline adherence and post-tonsillectomy admission practices is warranted. Keywords: Tonsillectomy, Adenotonsillectomy, Clinical practice guideline, Child, Obstructive sleep apnea, Admission criteria 1. Introduction Tonsillectomy with or without adenoidectomy is one of the most common surgical procedures performed in children under the age of 15, with 289,000 performed annually [ 1 ]. The most common indication is for treatment of obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) with significant impact on quality of life [ 2 ]. In the interest of identifying the most appropriate monitoring environment for children at the highest risk of postoperative complications following tonsillectomy for OSA, the American Academy of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery (AAO-HNS), the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), and the American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA), have all produced evidence-based clinical practice guidelines (CPGs) concerning management of children with OSA. The ASA guidelines [ 3 ] regarding perioperative considerations for children with obstructive sleep apnea were published in 2013 and recommend postoperative overnight monitoring for severe OSA as defined by Apnea Hypopnea Index (AHI) ≥10 [ 4 – 6 ]. The AAP’s most recent admission criteria, issued in 2012 [ 7 ], recommended a threshold of AHI ≥24 as a predictor of post-operative complications [ 8 ]. The AAP also added the recommendation of overnight hospital monitoring for SaO 2 (oxygen saturation) nadir <80% on preoperative polysomnography, citing its association with respiratory complications [ 9 ]. The most recently updated guidelines were issued by the AAO-HNS in 2019 [ 10 ]. While acknowledging a difference of opinion regarding the AHI cutoff and a lack of consensus on clinically significant severe OSA, the AAO-HNS CPG committee adopted a conservative cutoff of AHI ≥10, concurring with the ASA and other studies [ 3 , 11 – 13 ]. However, like the AAP, the updated AAO-HNS guidelines placed greater weight on hypoxia (O 2 saturation <80%) observed on preoperative polysomnography (PSG), citing evidence that, while AHI remained a significant predictor, O2 saturation was the single strongest predictor of postoperative complications [ 14 ]. Furthermore, AAP and AAO-HNS guidelines regarding when to consider polysomnography for diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea in children under consideration for adenotonsillectomy vary [ 15 ]. Thus, although the various guidelines put forth similar recommendations, there is no single standard concerning overnight hospital observation after pediatric tonsillectomy. This likely contributes to inconsistency in guideline implementation and adherence in many hospitals [ 16 ] despite research showing a significant decrease in unanticipated hospital admissions following implementation of guideline-concordant care [ 17 ]. Several factors could influence implementation, including consideration of the risks and drawbacks of hospitalization [ 18 ], skepticism regarding age as a primary criterion for hospital admission [ 19 , 20 ], costs associated with admission in strict adherence with the guidelines [ 21 ], and desire to incorporate wider variability of patient and provider factors [ 22 ]. Thus, it is not surprising that substantial variation in post-tonsillectomy admission practices among pediatric hospitals and clinical practice settings has been observed [ 23 ]. Overall, the factors associated with adherence to guidelines regarding overnight monitoring after tonsillectomy in children are unknown. The goal of this study is to understand the perspectives of individual practitioners in this regard. We hypothesized that admission practices would vary by practice setting, location, training, years of clinical experience, patient population, choice of tonsillectomy technique and method. 2. Materials and methods Our 20-question electronic survey (See Supplemental Fig. 1 ) consisted of a series of multiple-choice questions querying individual surgeons’ practice settings and tonsillectomy admission practices, with an open-ended question at the end to capture any further information that the respondents considered to be relevant. The survey was created, implemented, and compiled using SurveyMonkey, an online survey software company. Both the Einstein Institutional Review Board (IRB) and the American Society of Pediatric Otolaryngology (ASPO) Research Committee approved this study. The survey was electronically distributed to ASPO membership of 644 practicing ENTs across the country in July of 2021. They received an email containing a recruitment letter, assurance of confidentiality and the survey link. A second email containing the same items was sent one month later as a reminder. Data were analyzed by using SAS (version 9.4, Cary, NC) cross- tabulations of respondent characteristics versus patient factors and guideline adherence using Chi-square tests when assumptions were met. When expected frequencies were fewer than 5 in greater than 20% of cells, Fischer’s exact tests were used. For open-ended questions, the responses were analyzed qualitatively. Common themes were identified and clarifications of responses to closed-ended questions were noted. 3. Results The survey was completed by 123 practicing otolaryngologists with a response rate of 19.1% ( Table 1 ). The two largest groups of respondents had been in practice for over 21 years and 11–20 years, 34.5% and 30.2%, respectively, with a median of 11–20 years in practice for all respondents. The vast majority of respondents completed a pediatric otolaryngology fellowship (93.9%). Monthly tonsillectomy volume varied amongst respondents, with 42.2% reporting 11–20 such cases per month. 77.6% of respondents were in academic practice, with 81.0% performing surgery in a hospital setting and 19% in an ambulatory surgery center. Table 1. Demographics of respondents (n = 123). Responder Characteristics Response count (%) Length of practice ≤5 years 19 (16.4%) 6 – 10 years 20 (17.2%) 11 – 20 years 35 (30.2%) ≥21 years 40 (34.5%) Other (resident, fellow) 2 (1.7%) Pediatric fellowship Yes 108 (93.9%) No 7 (6.1%) Monthly T&A volume ≤10 15 (12.9%) 11 – 20 49 (42.2%) 21 – 30 34 (29.3%) 31 – 40 12 (10.3%) ≥41 6 (5.2%) Surgery setting Hospital 94 (81.0%) Ambulatory surgery center 22 (19.0%) Practice type Academic 90 (77.6%) Non-academic 26 (22.4%) Open in a new tab Several survey questions queried which criteria respondents used when considering observing children overnight following T&A ( Fig. 1 ). Overall, responses regarding age and AHI criteria aligned closely with CPG criteria, with 79.8% of respondents observing children under the age of 3 years overnight and 68.5% of respondents observing children with AHI ≥10. Approximately 13.8% of respondents used less strict age criteria (age less than 2 years) in their practice. A significant minority of respondents reported less strict AHI criteria (13.9% AHI ≥20, 1.9% AHI ≥24, 6.5% AHI ≥30). While nearly half of respondents used the CPG recommendation O2 nadir <80% as an admission criterion, similar proportions of respondents applied stricter thresholds, less strict thresholds, or indicated that O2 nadir does not regularly factor into their decision-making process. Fig. 1. Survey responses regarding admission criterion. Open in a new tab Percentage of respondents that selected each criterion for which they observe children overnight following tonsillectomy. The guideline concordant option (Age <3 years, Apnea Hypopnea Index ≥10, O2 nadir <80%) is indicated in red (lighter shading in non-Web versions of the article). Although obesity is not a criterion included in the AAO-HNS CPG, this factor was included in the survey due to its increasing prevalence in children. 83.4% of respondents regularly observed children overnight based on obesity. In analysis of open-ended questions throughout the survey, 5–10% of respondents volunteered comments regarding their own practices on each question, and 41% summarized their hospital’s policy. Many respondents commented on the importance of evaluating each patient individually and tailoring admission decisions to the unique circumstances of each case, for example, choosing to admit the patient for overnight observation depending on parent preference and the distance between the patient’s home and the hospital. Other respondents commented on additional hypoxia considerations such as the length of time observed with O2 below 90% and below 80% on polysomnogram. Still other respondents reported using stricter criteria than recommended by the CPGs, commenting that an AHI ≥1 prompted them to observe patients overnight. Comments reflected a variety of practices related to obesity, ranging from admitting all obese patients to those who favored admission of children with obesity if a sleep study had not been performed preoperatively. Respondents reported a high degree of familiarity with the AAO-HNS guidelines ( Fig. 2 ). 66.7% of respondents in practice for over 26 years reported that they ‘Often’ follow the guidelines while only 22.2% reported ‘Always’ following them, as compared to 53.7% and 37.8% respectively for those in practice for fewer years. Furthermore, respondents with more years in practice are more likely to use the American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) and the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) guidelines (See Supplemental Figs. 2 and 3 ). Fig. 2. Demographics of respondents according to AAO-HNS guideline adherence. Open in a new tab Demographic analysis based on respondents indicating their application of American Academy of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery (AAO-HNS) pediatric tonsillectomy guidelines in their practice. Respondents who had been in practice for 10 years or less were more likely to adhere to AHI≥ 10 (p = 0.03) and were more likely to adhere to the guidelines overall (p <0.01) ( Table 2 ). Furthermore, respondents who worked in an academic setting were more likely to adhere to the age criteria for admission (p = 0.01). Table 2. Correlation between respondent characteristics and CPG adherence for admission following pediatric adenotonsillectomy. Strict guideline adherence was defined as respondents who selected all three criteria of age <3 years, AHI ≥10 and O2 nadir <80%. AHI = Apnea Hypopnea Index, T&A = tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy. Characteristic Strict adherence to age criteria <3 yrs Strict adherence to AHI >10 Strict adherence to O2 nadir <80 Overall strict adherence (combining all categories) # (%) p-value # (%) p-value # (%) p-value # (%) p-value Length of practice 0–10 years 32 (88.9) 0.07 31 (86.1) 0.03 26 (72.2) 0.09 20 (55.6) <0.01 >10 years 53 (75.7) 42 (60.9) 37 (52.9) 16 (44.4) Monthly T&A Volume ≤20 47 (81.0) 0.09 40 (69.0) 0.95 36 (62.1) 0.2 19 (52.8) 0.77 >20 38 (79.2) 33 (70.21) 27 (56.3) 17 (47.2) Surgery Setting Hospital 17 (77.3) 0.29 16 (72.7) 0.54 14 (63.6) 0.57 9 (25.0) 0.44 Surgery Center 68 (81.0) 57 (68.7) 49 (58.3) 27 (75.0) Practice Type Academic 64 (79.0) 0.01 55 (68.75) 0.54 48 (59.26) 0.97 27 (75.0) 0.81 Non-academic 21 (84.0) 18 (72.0) 15 (60.0) 9 (25.0) Open in a new tab Looking at those with strict adherence overall, defined as those respondents who selected all three of the CPG criteria of age <3 years (80%), AHI ≥10 (69%) and O2 nadir <80% (50%), only 34% reported using all three. Demographically, years in practice was the only characteristic that was different (p < 0.01) between the strict and non-strict adherers; over half of respondents with less than 10 years in practice adhered strictly to the guidelines, while less than a quarter of those with more than 10 years’ experience indicated this ( Fig. 3 ). No differences were seen for T&A volume, T&A setting, or practice setting. Fig. 3. Characteristics of respondents who strictly adhered to admission criteria. Open in a new tab Characteristics of those who indicated strict adherence to the clinical practice guidelines, defined as those respondents who selected all three admission criteria of age <3 years, Apnea Hypopnea Index ≥10 and O2 nadir <80%. Patterns by AHI, O2 nadir and age criteria are summarized in Supplemental Figs. 4 – 6 . 4. Discussion There are several different CPGs to help pediatric otolaryngologists determine which patients should be observed overnight following tonsillectomy. We focused this survey on adherence to the most recent guidelines for tonsillectomy in children published in 2019 by the AAO-HNS. We hypothesized that admission practices would vary by practice setting, location, training, and years of clinical experience. Our results indicated that admission practices vary significantly by practice setting and years of clinical experience. Specifically, we found that years in practice consistently correlated with the different variables analyzed. Overall, there was a large amount of consensus among respondents regarding AHI criteria for overnight admission, indicating awareness and acceptance of the 2019 AAO-HNS CPG. However, some respondents reported familiarity with the AAP criteria instead, which uses a higher threshold for AHI and could explain the range of responses regarding that criterion. Indeed, the expert panel which created the CPG noted a lack of consensus regarding the AHI cutoff (10 vs. a higher AHI) in the literature, and that additional sleep study parameters may be useful to define severe OSA 10 . Among respondents, there was less agreement regarding O2 nadir thresholds for admission. This could reflect less adherence to CPGs, less consensus regarding where the cutoff should be, or heavier reliance on other variables to make decisions regarding overnight admission. Age criteria demonstrated the highest consensus of the admission criteria, with 80% of respondents indicating that they would admit children under the age of 3 for overnight observation following T&A. Furthermore, clinicians in an academic setting were significantly more likely to adhere to the age criteria. Surgery setting and surgeon volume were not associated with CPG adherence. Interestingly, most respondents indicated that they routinely admitted obese children regardless of other criteria, despite obesity not being one of the recommended criteria for admission, and reported using varying body mass index cutoffs. Given the increasing rates of obesity and the increased prevalence of OSA among obese individuals, these findings warrant further study and consideration of creating a recommendation for admission of patients with obesity. While 93% of those surveyed reported following AAO CPG often or always, only 34% strictly adhered to all 3 criteria, implying a gap between knowledge and implementation of CPGs. Particularly interesting is the finding that respondents who more strictly adhered to the CPGs had fewer years of clinical experience. Few studies have specifically investigated a relationship between years of experience and adherence to CPGs, but similar findings have been reported [ 24 ]. Further research into reasons for this finding is warranted. Part of the variance between knowledge and implementation may stem from the fact that each institution’s perioperative resources and post anesthesia observation protocols influence admission practices. Specifically, 66% of respondents commented in open-ended questions that patients near the CPG cutoff would be observed for 2–6 h in the recovery room before clinical determination of discharge home versus overnight observation. Other commenters responded that they make the decision based on their institution’s clinical discharge criteria, irrespective of time— “they leave when they are ready.” In addition to the associations between guideline adherence, practice setting, and years of clinical experience, the volume and breadth of comments submitted regarding different practices and deviations from CPGs warrant further exploration. Many physicians indicated being influenced by their own experience, and those who are familiar with the CPGs and their own institutional policy reported adapting them for their own practice. Our data suggests there is a large minority of otolaryngologists who have been practicing for a longer amount of time who apply less stringent thresholds to criteria than stated in the CPGs. Thus, discrepancies between guidelines and what clinicians are doing in real life warrant further study. Limitations to the study include the low response rate among the target population of pediatric otolaryngologists and the selection bias of respondents inherent in electronic survey-related studies. It is possible that ASPO members who held stronger opinions about these topics were more likely to answer the survey. In addition, it is acknowledged that children with comorbidities and the highest risk for complications are likely to be over-represented in the practices of children’s hospitals and academic practitioners, leading to a possible differential imperative to adhere to guidelines. Our study may be further confounded by the over-representation of later-career practitioners in private practice. Overall, our findings suggest that further development of CPGs to address implementation challenges and decrease the gap between guidelines and practice is warranted. Future directions include a prospective multi-institutional study with a quality improvement and implementation science approach; consideration of diverse practice settings; consideration of obesity as an additional criterion to determine need for overnight observation; and reassessment of the varying CPGs across pediatric societies for closer alignment. Supplementary Material Supp Fig 1 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_1.jpg (452.7KB, jpg) Supp Fig 2 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_2.jpg (563.9KB, jpg) Supp Fig 3 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_3.jpg (342KB, jpg) Supp Fig 4 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_4.jpg (353.9KB, jpg) Supp Fig 5 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_5.jpg (566.5KB, jpg) Supp Fig 6 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_6.jpg (503KB, jpg) Supp Fig 7 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_7.jpg (389.4KB, jpg) Supp Fig 8 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_8.jpg (436.9KB, jpg) Supp Fig 9 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_9.jpg (311.2KB, jpg) Supp Fig 10 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_10.jpg (282.5KB, jpg) Supp Fig 11 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_11.jpg (300.5KB, jpg) Supp Fig 12 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_12.jpg (527.7KB, jpg) Supp Fig 13 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_13.jpg (144.5KB, jpg) Supp Fig 14 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_14.jpg (143.6KB, jpg) Supp Fig 15 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_15.jpg (136.3KB, jpg) Supp Fig 16 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_16.jpg (139.2KB, jpg) Supp Fig 17 NIHMS1951810-supplement-Supp_Fig_17.jpg (128.9KB, jpg) Acknowledgements We would like to thank Maximilian Stahl, BS for his contributions to investigating variations in post-tonsillectomy admission practices at our institution. Dr. Yang is a Clinical Research Training Program scholar supported by NIH/National Center for Advancing Translational Science (NCATS) Einstein Montefiore CTSA (Clinical and Translational Science Awards) Grant Number UL1TR001073. Abbreviations: OSA Obstructive sleep apnea AAO-HNS American Academy of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery ASA American Society of Anesthesiologists AAP American Academy of Pediatrics CPG Clinical practice guideline AHI Apnea Hypopnea Index ASPO American Society of Pediatric Otolaryngology Footnotes Financial disclosures None. Appendix A. Supplementary data Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijporl.2022.111286 . References [1]. Hall MJ, Schwartzman A, Zhang J, Liu X, Ambulatory Surgery Data from Hospitals and Ambulatory Surgery Centers: United States, 2010, National health statistics reports, 2017, pp. 1–15. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Fehrm J, Nerfeldt P, Browaldh N, Friberg D, Effectiveness of adenotonsillectomy vs watchful waiting in young children with mild to moderate obstructive sleep apnea: a randomized clinical trial, JAMA Otolaryngol. Head Neck Surg 146 (2020) 647–654. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Patino M, Sadhasivam S, Mahmoud M, Obstructive sleep apnoea in children: perioperative considerations, Br. J. Anaesth 111 (2